var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/3-reactant/#3.-Compiling-with-Reactant","page":"Compiling with Reactant","text":"Loading Reactant.jl enables the package extension LuxTriangleAttentionReactantExt, and every layer can then be compiled with @compile (or run with @jit) to an XLA program, on the CPU or, through the jax-mps PJRT plugin, on an Apple GPU. The layers need no changes: they are written so the traced version is the same code.\n\nusing Lux, LuxTriangleAttention, Reactant, Random\n\ndev = Reactant.to_rarray\nattn = TriangleAttention(128, 32, 4)\nps, st = Lux.setup(Random.default_rng(), attn)\nx = randn(Float32, 128, 64, 64, 1)\n\nf(l, x, ps, st) = first(l(x, ps, st))\ncompiled = @compile f(attn, dev(x), dev(ps), dev(st))\ny = Array(compiled(attn, dev(x), dev(ps), dev(st)))\n\nGradients go through Enzyme as usual (Enzyme.gradient(Reverse, loss, ...) inside the compiled function). The layers' token-major layout, which makes the contractions plain batched matrix products, was chosen by measuring under Reactant, where it is up to roughly 2× faster than the channel-first formulation.","title":"3. Compiling with Reactant"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/3-reactant/#Bounding-memory-with-chunk_size","page":"Compiling with Reactant","text":"Attention(...; chunk_size=n) runs the attention in slices of n along dimension 4 of the prepared q, k, v. Attention runs over dimension 3, so dimension 4 is the first axis it batches over, and for a pair input [C, Ni, Nj, B] that is Nj, a token axis, not the sample batch. The live score tensor shrinks from [Nq, Nk, H, Nj, B] to chunk_size slices of it, and it does so at B == 1. (For a 3-D input [C, N, B] dimension 4 is the batch, so chunk_size does nothing at B == 1.) This is chunk_size_tri_attn of OpenFold and Boltz.\n\nattn = TriangleAttention(128, 32, 4; chunk_size=16)\n\nEagerly on the CPU this is an ordinary loop and bounds memory as you would expect (3.2 GB less peak for about 12 % more time on one Pairformer layer at N = 512). On a GPU it is the wrong tool, because a fused kernel never materialises the scores in the first place: the layer warns once, and use_kernel is what you want.","title":"Bounding memory with chunk_size"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/3-reactant/#Keeping-the-loop-rolled","page":"Compiling with Reactant","text":"Inside @compile the Julia loop would be unrolled into the graph, and because the chunks are independent XLA is free to run them concurrently and keep every slice live, which cancels the benefit. The extension therefore replaces the loop with a rolled @trace loop, a stablehlo.while. Emitting the while is still not enough: two Enzyme-HLO passes can turn it back into the unchunked graph.\n\ngreedy_while_loop_batch_fission sees the iterations are independent and fuses them into one batched dot_general. It fires at any chunk count.\nthe while-loop unroller fires when nchunks <= Reactant.Compiler.WHILE_UNROLL_THRESHOLD[] (default 4).\n\nchunked_compile_options holds off the first, and the threshold is a global you set yourself:\n\nReactant.Compiler.WHILE_UNROLL_THRESHOLD[] = 0\nopts = LuxTriangleAttention.chunked_compile_options()\ncompiled = @compile compile_options=opts f(attn, dev(x), dev(ps), dev(st))\n\nCheck that it worked, rather than assuming, by looking at the optimised module:\n\nmod = @code_hlo compile_options=opts f(attn, dev(x), dev(ps), dev(st))\nLuxTriangleAttention.check_rolled(mod)       # true: a `stablehlo.while` is still there\n\nThe numbers are identical either way, which is how an unrolled, memory-hungry program goes unnoticed. The threshold matters most for the reverse pass: Reactant's checkpointing nests the loop in two, with trip counts of order sqrt(nchunks) that fall under the default threshold at essentially any chunk count, and the layer warns once if the threshold is not 0. On some Reactant versions an unrolled reverse does not even compile.\n\nwarning: Verify the reverse before relying on a chunk size\nDifferentiating the rolled loop hits an unfixed Reactant/Enzyme defect for some configurations: the chunk write becomes a dynamic_update_slice with an update one rank too many (update rank does not match operand rank), which aborts the process. The forward is unaffected. No rule predicts it; on a bare Attention (chn = 32, head_dim = 8, 4 heads) it was the same at every N tried:nchunks 2 3 4 5 6\nreverse ✗ ✗ ✓ ✗ ✓A given nchunks may work or fail depending on the surrounding layer. Try the gradient before committing to one.\n\nChunk sizes that do not divide the axis fall back to the unrolled form, with the same result.","title":"Keeping the loop rolled"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/3-reactant/#Fused-kernels-in-a-compiled-program","page":"Compiling with Reactant","text":"With use_kernel=true and a Metal client the kernel is embedded as a stablehlo.custom_call that jax-mps runs. This needs the Metal PJRT client to be the default (Reactant's own default is the CPU client, under which a traced array is a host array and the layer takes the host path):\n\nusing Reactant, Metal\nReactant.set_default_backend(client)         # the jax-mps client, see test/mps_client.jl\n\nTwo things to know:\n\nTune ahead of @compile. Compiling a kernel is eager device work, which the tracer would capture, so a missing variant cannot be built mid-trace and the compile throws, naming the call to run. tune_flash_kernel! (or tune_flash_mma!) once, for the head dimension and storage type you compile with; any lengths will do, since a call runs the closest tuned one. See Fused attention kernels.\nForward only. A gradient traced through a kernel throws until Enzyme-JAX can differentiate a custom call (EnzymeAD/Enzyme#2516). To train under Reactant use the unfused layer (optionally with chunk_size), and use the kernel for inference. You can still call the kernels' backward directly.","title":"Fused kernels in a compiled program"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/3-reactant/#Float16","page":"Compiling with Reactant","text":"A rolled chunk runs in Float32 inside Float16 programs. The compiler may move the 1/√d scale past Q·Kᵀ, and the unscaled logits reach 9e4 on Boltz2's activations, past floatmax(Float16) = 65504. The plain path (no chunking, no kernel) is LuxLib's attention, which is not Float16-safe under Reactant and materialises every score: it is for reference and tests. Use chunk_size or use_kernel for 16-bit data. More in Half precision.","title":"Float16"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API reference","text":"Every exported and public name. Names starting with an underscore are internal and may change; they are listed on the Internals page.","title":"API reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Layers","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Layers"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Masks-and-bias","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Masks and bias"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Normalisation","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Normalisation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Precision","page":"API reference","text":"Public, not exported.","title":"Precision"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Chunked-attention","page":"API reference","text":"Both need Reactant loaded.","title":"Chunked attention"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Attention-kernels","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Attention kernels"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Tuning","page":"API reference","text":"","title":"Tuning"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Other","page":"API reference","text":"backend_of, which says which backend an array belongs to, is KernelDispatch': see its documentation.\n\nEverything else in the package, such as the tuner, the stage launcher and the kernel sources, is on the Internals page.","title":"Other"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.TriangleAttention","page":"API reference","text":"TriangleAttention(chn_in, head_dim, num_heads; is_starting=true, kwargs...)\n\nImplementation of Triangle Attention (AlphaFold2 Algorithms 13 & 14). Updates the pair representation by performing axial attention with a triangle bias. Per head h, AlphaFold2 Algorithm 13 (starting node) is\n\nbeginaligned\nz_ij leftarrow mathrmLayerNorm(z_ij) \nq^h_ij k^h_ij v^h_ij = mathrmLinearNoBias(z_ij) qquad\nb^h_ij = mathrmLinearNoBias(z_ij) qquad\ng^h_ij = mathrmsigmoidbig(mathrmLinear(z_ij)big) \na^h_ijk = mathrmsoftmax_kBig(tfrac1sqrtc q^h_ij^top k^h_ik + b^h_jkBig) \no^h_ij = g^h_ij odot sum_k a^h_ijk v^h_ik \ntildez_ij = mathrmLinearbig(mathrmconcat_h(o^h_ij)big)\nendaligned\n\nand AlphaFold2 Algorithm 14 (ending node) uses k^h_kj, b^h_ki and v^h_kj in place of k^h_ik, b^h_jk and v^h_ik. c is head_dim.\n\nArguments\n\nchn_in: Input channels.\nhead_dim: Dimension of each attention head.\nnum_heads: Number of attention heads.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nis_starting: If true, performs \"Starting Node\" attention (AlphaFold2 Algorithm 13). If false, performs \"Ending Node\" attention (AlphaFold2 Algorithm 14).\nuse_bias: Boolean or NamedTuple for internal layer biases.\nlayernorm_eps: Epsilon for LayerNorm.\nrank: The rank (ndims) of the input.\nRemaining kwargs are forwarded to the inner Attention — notably chunk_size, which bounds the O(N³·H) attention-score footprint by attending over slices of the batch axis.\n\nInputs\n\nx: Input tensor with shape [C, Ni, Nj, B].\nmask: Optional mask with shape [Ni, Nj, B].\n\nThe triangle bias is NOT a user-supplied input: linear projects it from x itself (b = Linear(x_norm), shape [H, Ni, Nj, B]). It is then handed to the inner Attention with bias_layout=:qk, which calls prep_bias on it internally.\n\nReturns\n\ny: Output tensor with shape [C, Ni, Nj, B].\nst: Updated state.\n\nExample\n\nusing Lux, LuxTriangleAttention, Random\n\nmodel = TriangleAttention(64, 32, 4; is_starting=true)\nps, st = Lux.setup(Random.default_rng(), model)\n\nx = randn(Float32, 64, 32, 32, 1)\nmask = rand(Bool, 32, 32, 1) # Pairwise boolean mask\n\n# Pass inputs via NamedTuple; the bias is computed internally, not supplied here\ny, st = model((; x, mask), ps, st)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.TriangleAttention"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.TriangleMultiplication","page":"API reference","text":"TriangleMultiplication(chn_in, chn_hidden; is_outgoing=true, kwargs...)\n\nImplementation of Triangle Multiplication (AlphaFold2 Algorithms 11 & 12). Updates the pair representation by combining edges sharing a common node. AlphaFold2 Algorithm 11 (outgoing edges) is\n\nbeginaligned\nz_ij leftarrow mathrmLayerNorm(z_ij) \na_ij b_ij = mathrmsigmoidbig(mathrmLinear(z_ij)big) odot mathrmLinear(z_ij) \ng_ij = mathrmsigmoidbig(mathrmLinear(z_ij)big) \ntildez_ij = g_ij odot mathrmLinearBig(mathrmLayerNormbig(textstylesum_k a_ik odot b_jkbig)Big)\nendaligned\n\nand AlphaFold2 Algorithm 12 (incoming edges) sums a_ki odot b_kj instead.\n\nArguments\n\nchn_in: Input channels.\nchn_hidden: Hidden channels for internal projections.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nis_outgoing: If true, performs \"Outgoing\" multiplication (AlphaFold2 Algorithm 11). If false, performs \"Incoming\" multiplication (AlphaFold2 Algorithm 12).\nlayernorm_eps: Epsilon for LayerNorm.\nrank: The rank (ndims) of the input.\nkwargs...: Passed to TriMulCore.\n\nInputs\n\nx: Input tensor with shape [C, N, N, B].\nmask: Optional mask with shape [N, N, B].\n\nReturns\n\ny: Output tensor with shape [C, N, N, B].\nst: Updated state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.TriangleMultiplication"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.Attention","page":"API reference","text":"Attention(chn_q, chn_k, chn_v, head_dim, num_heads; use_bias=false, fuse_qkv=true,\n    use_gate=static(true), bias_layout=:kq, chunk_size=nothing, use_kernel=static(false))\nAttention(chn_q, chn_kv, head_dim, num_heads; kwargs...)\nAttention(chn_in, head_dim, num_heads; kwargs...)\n\nMulti-head attention layer. Supports self-attention, cross-attention, and gated attention.\n\nArguments\n\nchn_q: Channels for the query input.\nchn_k: Channels for the key input.\nchn_v: Channels for the value input.\nhead_dim: Dimension of each attention head.\nnum_heads: Number of attention heads.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nuse_bias: A NamedTuple or Bool specifying which internal layers should use bias.  Defaults to false.\nfuse_qkv: If true, fuses the Q, K, and V projections into a single dense layer  (where possible).\nuse_gate: If true (or static(true)), applies a sigmoid gating to the attention output.\nuse_kernel: If true (or static(true)), routes attention through the default (or user-defined)  fused kernel (see use_kernel! / tune_flash_kernel!)  instead of Lux.scaled_dot_product_attention. Defaults to false.\nThis only takes effect on GPU arrays. On CPU, SDPA always runs the ordinary softmax regardless of this flag. A model built with use_kernel=true still runs (correctly,  just without the speedup); the flag applies the moment the arrays are actually on a device with a  usable kernel selected.\nMutually exclusive with chunk_size: a fused kernel tiles the keys internally, so there is nothing left for chunk_size to slice, and combining the two raises AssertionError at construction.\nbias_layout: A Symbol (either :qk or :kq) indicating how the attention bias tensor  should be prepared internally. Defaults to :kq.\n:qk: Used in TriangleAttention. Expects a bias of shape [H, Ni, Nj, B], which is  permuted to [Ni, Nj, H, 1, B] to match the Julia Lux 3rd-dimension attention default.  Since the input is also permuted in TriangleAttention, no additional transpose of the  bias is required.\n:kq: Used in pair-bias implementations (e.g., MSA or general pair representations).  Expects a bias of shape [H, Nq, Nk, B], which is permuted/transposed to [Nk, Nq, H, 1, B]  to correct for the difference between Julia Lux's 3rd-dimension attention and Python's 4th-dimension attention when working with inputs of shape [C, N, S, B] (vs Python's [B, S, N, C]).\nchunk_size: If given, run the attention in slices of chunk_size along dim 4 of the prepared q/k/v instead of all at once. Defaults to nothing (no chunking). The result is unchanged but the  live score tensor shrinks from [Nq, Nk, H, dim4, …] to chunk_size entries at a time.\nAttention runs over token_dim=3, so dim 4 is the first axis it batches over and for a pair input  [C, Ni, Nj, B] that is Nj, a token axis, not the sample batch. Chunking therefore helps at  B == 1, whose scores are O(N³·H), ~1 GiB per op at N=400. For a 3-D input [C, N, B] dim 4 is  the sample batch, so chunk_size is a no-op at B == 1. Equivalent to chunk_size_tri_attn in the  openfold/boltz references.\nFloat16. The chunked paths are Float16-safe, eagerly on the CPU and rolled under Reactant (a chunk runs in Float32 there, since the compiler may move the 1/√d scale past Q·Kᵀ and overflow the logits). The plain path (chunk_size=nothing, use_kernel=false) is LuxLib's SDPA, which is not Float16-safe under Reactant and materialises every score; it is for reference and tests, not production. Use chunk_size or use_kernel.\nUnder Reactant.@compile the Reactant extension rolls the chunk loop into a stablehlo.while; the plain Julia loop here would be unrolled and scheduled concurrently, preventing any reductions in  memory usage.\nwarning: Verify the REVERSE before relying on a specific chunk size\nDifferentiating the rolled loop hits an unfixed Reactant/Enzyme defect for some configurations: the chunk write lowers to a dynamic_update_slice whose update has one rank too many (update rank does not match operand rank: 6 vs 5). The FORWARD is unaffected.No rule predicts it. Measured on a bare Attention (chn=32, headdim=8, numheads=4), the same at every N tried (8, 12, 16, 20, 24):nchunks 2 3 4 5 6\nreverse ✗ ✗ ✓ ✗ ✓A specific nchunks might work or fail depending on the surrounding layer (e.g. a PairFormer block featuring TriangleAttention layers). Eliminated as causes: the chunked axis's position and rank, the loop body, the number of carried buffers, mask, bias, num_heads, and absolute N.\n\nInputs\n\nx: Input tensor(s). Can be an AbstractArray for self-attention, or a Tuple of  arrays for cross-attention (e.g., (q, kv) or (q, k, v)).  Expected shape: [C, N, (N or S, ) B].\nbias: Optional attention bias tensor. Expected shape: [num_heads, Nq, Nk, B]. Must have the correct shape for broadcasting. See prep_bias.\nmask: Optional attention mask. Expected shape: [Nq, (S, ) B] or [Ni, Nj, B]. Masks are automatically reshaped to the internal 4D/5D attention score format.\n\nReturns\n\ny: The output tensor. Shape matches q (typically [chn_q, N, (N or S, ) B]).\nst: Updated state.\n\nExample\n\nusing Lux, LuxTriangleAttention, Random\n\n# Self-attention with mask\nmodel = Attention(64, 32, 4)\nps, st = Lux.setup(Random.default_rng(), model)\n\nx = randn(Float32, 64, 32, 1)\nmask = rand(Bool, 32, 1)\n\n# Pass inputs as a NamedTuple\ny, st = model((; x, mask), ps, st)\n\nNote on Dimensions\n\nThis implementation performs attention over the 3rd dimension (token_dim=3). This is different from most Python references (e.g., Boltz-2, AlphaFold2 and 3) which  typically attend over the 4th dimension.\n\nTriangleAttention (bias_layout=:qk): In TriangleAttention, we pass a bias of shape [H, Ni, Nj, B] which is permuted internally to enable the model to attend to the 3rd dimension in q, k, v (representing Nj). The input is of size [C, Ni, Nj, B]. Permuting the input also permutes the resulting bias correctly, meaning no additional changes to the bias orientation are needed.\nGeneric pair-bias attention (bias_layout=:kq), matching the layout of the AttentionPairBias module in the AlphaFold3/Boltz Python references. For generic pair bias attention, the input is of shape [C, N, S, B] (where S is the pair/MSA dimension), and we attend over the N dimension. In Python, the equivalent shape is [B, S, N, C]. While the Julia input layout works naturally with Lux's 3rd-dimension attention default, the bias tensor of shape [H, Nq, Nk, B] must be permuted to [Nk, Nq, H, 1, B] to correct for the transposition. This is handled by the :kq layout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.Attention"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.TriMulCore","page":"API reference","text":"TriMulCore(chn_in, chn_hidden; is_outgoing=true, fused=true, kwargs...)\n\nCore logic for Triangle Multiplication. Handles projections, masking, and  batched matrix multiplication for edge updates.\n\nArguments\n\nchn_in: Input channels.\nchn_hidden: Hidden channels.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nis_outgoing: If true, performs \"Outgoing\" multiplication.\nfused: If true, fuses the A & B projections into a single GLU operation.\nfused_glu: If true, uses fused GLU layers.\nuse_bias: Boolean for internal layer biases.\nrank: The rank (ndims) of the input.\n\nInputs\n\nx: Input tensor with shape [C, N, N, B].\nmask: Optional mask with shape [N, N, B].\n\nReturns\n\ny: Output tensor with shape [chn_in, N, N, B].\nst: Updated state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.TriMulCore"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.GatedLinearUnit","page":"API reference","text":"GatedLinearUnit(in_chn => out_chn; fused=static(true), activation=Lux.sigmoid, use_bias=false)\nGatedLinearUnit((in_val, in_gate) => out_chn; ...)\n\nImplements a Gated Linear Unit: y = linear(x_linear) * activation(gate(x_gate)).\n\nArguments\n\nin_out: A Pair mapping input channels to output channels. Can also be (in_val, in_gate) => out_chn.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nfused: If true, in_val must equal in_gate, and a single Dense call is used for both projections.\nactivation: Activation function to apply to the gate branch.\nuse_bias: Whether to use bias in the linear projections.\n\nInputs\n\nx: Input tensor with shape [C, N, ...] or a Tuple of tensors for dual inputs.\n\nReturns\n\ny: Output tensor with shape [out_chn, N, ...].\nst: Updated state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.GatedLinearUnit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.SwiGLU","page":"API reference","text":"SwiGLU(in_chn => out_chn; kwargs...)\n\nA variant of GatedLinearUnit using the swish activation function.  Commonly used in Transformer architectures (e.g., LLaMA, Boltz-1).\n\nArguments\n\nin_out: Input to Output mapping.\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nkwargs...: Passed to GatedLinearUnit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.SwiGLU"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.ChannelsLast","page":"API reference","text":"ChannelsLast(layer)\n\nlayer applied to CHANNELS-LAST data: input [..., in], output [..., out], where Lux's own convention is channels-first ([in, ...]). Supported: Lux.Dense, Lux.LayerNorm (normalising the channel axis only) and GatedLinearUnit/SwiGLU. Any other layer throws.\n\nThe parameters and states are the wrapped layer's own, unnested (an AbstractLuxWrapperLayer), so ChannelsLast(Dense(chn => hidden)) has exactly the parameter tree of Dense(chn => hidden): the same initialisation, the same weight loading.\n\nWhy\n\nA Dense on channels-last data computes x · Wᵀ instead of W · x, and the result is channels-last too. Inside a layer this is often cheaper:\n\na channels-last [N², H] projection IS the per-channel [N, N] \"planes\" layout a batched contraction over tokens wants (e.g. TriMulCore), so no permutedims is needed before it;\nunder Reactant → jax-mps, channels-last chains lower to row-major dot_generals, which jax-mps runs 1.1–1.9× faster (docs/FEATUREjaxmpsdot_orientation.md §3b).\n\nWhether it pays off is a per-layer measurement, not a rule: see test/scratch/layout_harness.jl.\n\nEntering and leaving the layout\n\nA 2-D input may be a lazy transpose of channels-first data: ChannelsLast(d)(transpose(x), ps, st) computes xᵀ · Wᵀ directly, with no copy. The way back is one permutedims at the end of the layer.\n\nExample\n\nd = ChannelsLast(Lux.Dense(128 => 512))\nps, st = Lux.setup(rng, d)                 # same as for Lux.Dense(128 => 512)\ny, _ = d(randn(Float32, 64, 64, 128), ps, st)   # [64, 64, 512]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.ChannelsLast"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.prep_mask","page":"API reference","text":"prep_mask(mask)\n\nUtility to reshape various mask shapes into the internal 4D/5D format expected by  Lux.scaled_dot_product_attention.\n\nArguments\n\nmask: Input mask.\n\nReturns\n\nSupported conversions:\n\n[N, B] -> [N, 1, 1, B]\n[Ni, Nj, B] -> [Ni, 1, 1, Nj, B]\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.prep_mask"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.prep_bias","page":"API reference","text":"prep_bias(bias, x, bias_layout)\n\nUtility to prepare and reshape the attention bias tensor based on the input shape and the specified bias_layout.\n\nArguments\n\nbias: The raw bias tensor (or nothing).\nx: The input tensor to the attention layer.\nbias_layout: A StaticSymbol specifying the permutation strategy (:qk or :kq).\n\nPermutation Strategies\n\n:qk (Symmetric/Triangle Attention): For an input of shape [C, Ni, Nj, B] and bias of shape [H, Ni, Nj, B], permutes the bias to [Ni, Nj, H, 1, B] (preserving the spatial dimensions Ni and Nj).\n:kq (Asymmetric/Pair Attention): For an input of shape [C, N, S, B] and bias of shape [H, Nq, Nk, B], permutes the bias to [Nk, Nq, H, 1, B] (transposing spatial dimensions to correct for Julia's 3rd-dimension attention vs Python's 4th-dimension attention).\n\nNotes on Dimensions\n\nPython packages (such as AlphaFold and Boltz) typically perform attention over the 4th dimension (using [B, S, N, C]). Julia's Lux default is to attend over the 3rd dimension ([C, N, S, B]). The bias_layout determines how the bias tensor is adjusted to maintain parity with the Python attention mechanisms under these different dimension conventions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.prep_bias"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.StableLayerNorm","page":"API reference","text":"StableLayerNorm(shape, activation=identity; epsilon=1f-5, dims=Colon(), affine=True(), kwargs...)\nStableLayerNorm(layer)    # a Lux.LayerNorm, or one frozen with `Lux.Experimental.freeze`\n\nA Lux.LayerNorm whose statistics are always accumulated in Float32 when the data is 16-bit.\n\nFor Float32 and Float64 (and any non-16-bit input, traced or not) it IS the wrapped Lux.LayerNorm: the call is layer(x, ps, st), so whatever Lux and LuxLib dispatch on for the array type — today a generic AbstractArray method that traced Reactant arrays flow through, and any AnyTracedRArray method a later Lux adds — is used unchanged, and the result is bitwise the layer's own.\n\nFor Float16 and BFloat16 it computes the mean and variance in Float32 (layernorm_f32stats) and returns the result in the input's type, with the layer's own scale, bias, activation, dims and epsilon. Lux.LayerNorm accumulates in the input type, where the sum of squares behind the variance overflows Float16 at |x| ≈ 20 over a few hundred channels (σ² ≈ 2e6 on AlphaFold2's pair activations is not representable): finite but 85–99% wrong output on pair activations with σ ≈ 60 over 32 channels.\n\nThe parameters and state are the wrapped layer's own, unnested (an AbstractLuxWrapperLayer), so StableLayerNorm(shape) has exactly the parameter tree of Lux.LayerNorm(shape): the same initialisation, the same weight loading. ChannelsLast supports it too (for the unfrozen layer).\n\nThe wrapped layer may be a Lux.LayerNorm frozen with Lux.Experimental.freeze (that is what LayerNormNoBias builds): its frozen parameters live in the state, and the Float32 path reads them from there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.StableLayerNorm"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.LayerNormNoBias","page":"API reference","text":"LayerNormNoBias(dims; epsilon=1f-5, kwargs...)\n\nA StableLayerNorm (a Lux.LayerNorm, Float16-safe) where the bias is frozen at zero. A factory function: the result is a StableLayerNorm wrapping the frozen layer, with the parameter and state tree the frozen layer has (ps = (; scale), the zero bias in st.frozen_params). This is useful for  architectures where affine transformation is desired but without a learnable bias term.\n\nArguments\n\nSee Lux.LayerNorm\n\nKeyword Arguments\n\nkwargs...: Passed to Lux.LayerNorm.\n\nInputs\n\nx: Input tensor.\n\nReturns\n\ny: Normalized tensor.\nst: Updated state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.LayerNormNoBias"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.layernorm_f32stats","page":"API reference","text":"layernorm_f32stats(x, scale, bias, activation, dims, epsilon, ::Type{T})\n\nNormalise x over dims with the mean and variance accumulated in Float32, in ONE pass (σ² = E[x²] − μ²), and return the result in T.\n\nFor 16-bit inputs the statistics cannot stay in the input type: the sum of squares over a few hundred channels overflows Float16 at |x| ≈ 20, and σ² itself (≈2e6 on AlphaFold2's pair activations) is not representable. One pass is safe here although it is not for Float32 inputs: for 16-bit data accumulated in Float32 its cancellation error stays below the input's own rounding until |μ|/σ ≈ 2¹⁴. Only μ and 1/√(σ²+ε) are converted back, so a 16-bit x is normalised in T without a Float32 copy; a Float32 x (a contraction computed wide) is normalised in Float32 and then converted. epsilon is used in Float32, so it may be far below T's range. scale/bias (or nothing) must already be broadcast-compatible with x; they are narrowed to T if wider.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.layernorm_f32stats"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.ishalf","page":"API reference","text":"ishalf(T::Type) -> Bool\n\nWhether T is a 16-bit floating-point type that needs the ContractionMode handling: Float16, BFloat16 (BFloat16s.jl's, which Metal.jl and Reactant use) and Core.BFloat16.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.ishalf"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.mask_value","page":"API reference","text":"mask_value(T::Type) -> T\nmask_value(x::AbstractArray) -> eltype\n\nThe value to put where attention is masked: -floatmax(T) / 2, FINITE in every precision. A masked logit written as -Inf (what typemin is for a float, and what LuxLib fills in), or as a large constant that overflows T, turns a row with no live key into -Inf - (-Inf) = NaN; OpenFold's and Boltz2's 1e9 * (mask - 1) is finite in Float32 but IS -Inf in Float16 (Float16(-1e9) == -Inf), which is how a fully padded window gave NaN there. Use it by SELECTING, not adding:\n\nlogits = ifelse.(keep, logits, mask_value(T))\n\nA select needs only that the value is finite, so it holds in any precision, and a row where every logit is mask_value is a uniform softmax (the behaviour of a fully masked row everywhere in this package, see SDPA). As an additive bias it still fits T for logits of magnitude below floatmax(T) / 2 (about 3.3e4 in Float16); a select is the safe form. exp(mask_value(T) - m) is exactly 0 for any row maximum m a model can produce, so masked keys get no weight in a row that has a live one.\n\nBFloat16 (Core.BFloat16, and Reactant's) has Float32's exponent range and gets Float32's value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.mask_value"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.fold_k","page":"API reference","text":"fold_k(L::Integer) -> Int\n\nThe scale exponent for a contraction of length up to L: the smallest k with 4ᵏ ≥ L. Folding 2⁻ᵏ into each of the two projections divides the contraction by 4ᵏ, so it sums roughly a mean whose size no longer grows with L. A k larger than needed only leaves values smaller, which Float16 represents at full relative precision down to 6e-5, so L is a BOUND (the longest contraction the parameters must serve), not the length of any particular input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.fold_k"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.fold_trimul","page":"API reference","text":"fold_trimul(ps, st, k) -> (ps, st)\n\nPrepare a TriangleMultiplication's parameters for the Folded path: scale the VALUE projections of a and b (never the gates) by 2⁻ᵏ and record fold_k = k in the core's state. Apply it to the Float32 parameters, before casting to 16 bits (the scaling is a power of two, so it is exact either way, but subnormal weights would round). Use fold_k(L) for the longest sequence the parameters must serve.\n\nThe contraction then sums Σ a·b / 4ᵏ, and the output LayerNorm (scale-invariant, with ε scaled by 16⁻ᵏ in the layer) makes the layer's result exactly the unscaled one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.fold_trimul"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.ContractionMode","page":"API reference","text":"ContractionMode\n\nHow a layer with a long contraction runs, chosen from its input's element type and its state: Folded, HalfSafe or FullPrecision (see src/precision.jl).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.ContractionMode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.FullPrecision","page":"API reference","text":"Parameters not pre-scaled, data not 16-bit: the ordinary code path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.FullPrecision"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.HalfSafe","page":"API reference","text":"16-bit data, parameters NOT pre-scaled: the contraction runs in Float32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.HalfSafe"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.Folded","page":"API reference","text":"The contraction's projections pre-scaled by 2⁻ᵏ (st.fold_k == k), any element type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.Folded"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.contraction_mode","page":"API reference","text":"contraction_mode(x::AbstractArray, st) -> ContractionMode\n\nFolded(st.fold_k) if the state carries one; else HalfSafe if x holds 16-bit numbers (traced or not); else FullPrecision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.contraction_mode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.chunked_compile_options","page":"API reference","text":"chunked_compile_options(; kwargs...)\n\nReactant.CompileOptions under which a chunk_sized attention stays rolled rather than being optimized back into the unchunked graph. Requires Reactant to be loaded; the method in LuxTriangleAttentionReactantExt documents which passes it holds off and why.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.chunked_compile_options"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.check_rolled","page":"API reference","text":"check_rolled(mod)\n\nWhether an optimized @code_hlo module still contains a stablehlo.while — i.e. whether the chunking actually survived. Requires Reactant to be loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.check_rolled"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.SDPA","page":"API reference","text":"SDPA\n\nThe attention op: SDPA()(q, k, v, mask, bias) -> (o, lse). q, k, v are [hd, H, Nq, Nj, B] (or a plain [hd, H, N, B] sequence), mask a KEEP mask and bias the pair bias, both optional. Host arrays run _sdpa_default (lse = nothing); device arrays the selected kernel — use_kernel!(SDPA, :flash_mma).\n\nA fully masked key set\n\nIf every key of an (outer index, batch) slice is masked, its queries attend UNIFORMLY, as in OpenFold and Boltz2 (whose finite 1e9·(mask - 1) bias makes all logits equal): o is the mean of v over the n keys, never NaN. The reverse gives dV = dO / n to every key and nothing to q, k or bias (the logits were replaced). Every path follows it: _sdpa_default, the rolled chunk path and both kernels, which write lse = -Inf for such a query as the marker their backward reads. Only a WHOLE slice is dead this way; a single query made all -Inf by its own bias is not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.SDPA"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.use_kernel!","page":"API reference","text":"use_kernel!(name)\n\nSelect the attention kernel for the rest of the session (use_kernel!(SDPA, name)). The selection is a method, so it takes effect from the NEXT top-level expression: an attention call later in the same function or block throws instead of running the previous kernel. To run one call with a particular kernel, call it directly: KernelDispatch.forward(SDPA(), LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA(), q, k, v, mask, bias).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.use_kernel!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.current_kernel","page":"API reference","text":"current_kernel(): the selected attention kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.current_kernel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.list_kernels","page":"API reference","text":"list_kernels(): the attention kernels, with their variant counts and the selected one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.list_kernels"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.device_kernel_available","page":"API reference","text":"device_kernel_available() -> Bool\n\nWhether a backend extension is loaded AND its device is usable (KernelDispatch.device_available()). False on a host-only setup.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.device_kernel_available"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.tune_flash_kernel!","page":"API reference","text":"tune_flash_kernel!(lengths=tune_lengths(); dim, T=Float32, force=false) -> variants\n\ntune!(FlashScalar(), lengths; dim, T, force): tune, compile and register this package's own flash kernel :default at each sequence length in lengths (an integer or a collection; default tune_lengths). A call of any length runs the closest. Requires a backend extension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.tune_flash_kernel!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.tune_flash_mma!","page":"API reference","text":"tune_flash_mma!(lengths=tune_lengths(); dim, T=Float32, force=false) -> variants\n\ntune!(FlashMMA(), lengths; dim, T, force): tune, compile and register the matrix-unit kernel :flash_mma at each length in lengths; select it with use_kernel!(:flash_mma). A call of any length runs the closest tuned one; an eager call with none tunes tune_lengths lazily, a traced one refuses. dim must be a multiple of 8, and of the device's contraction width (8 on Metal, 16 on NVIDIA) to run on the matrix units; other multiples of 8 run :default. Where the device computes T at another type (Float32 at Float16 on CUDA, see mma_compute_type) that type is tuned; where it has none, a call runs :default and this returns without tuning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.tune_flash_mma!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.calibrate!","page":"API reference","text":"calibrate!(kernel, T=Float32; dim, n, force=false, probe_heads=4, persist=true)\n    -> Dict(stage => Dict(point => seconds))\n\nMeasure every stage of a built-in kernel on this device at sequence length n: the admissible points of its tune_grid, searched (search) and each measured — built, admitted by its pipeline, verified, timed on a triangle probe of n queries (nouter = n, capped for memory) and probe_heads head slices. Persisted (calibration_path), so a later call is a file read; force=true re-measures.\n\nKeyed by (kernel, device, dim, T, n): T is the STORAGE type, part of the key because it changes which configurations are legal (the staged tiles are held in storage precision), not merely how fast they are. A stage with no finite point is an error — there is then no kernel to run.\n\n\n\n\n\ncalibrate!([T=Float32]; dim, n=first(tune_lengths()), force=false) -> Dict(stage => Dict(point => seconds))\n\ncalibrate!(FlashScalar(), T; dim, n, force): measure :default on this device for (dim, T) at length n and persist it, so a later call is a file read. Run it deliberately: it compiles, verifies and times candidate configurations for all four stages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.calibrate!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.calibrate_mma!","page":"API reference","text":"calibrate_mma!([T=Float32]; dim, n=first(tune_lengths()), force=false) -> Dict(stage => Dict(point => seconds))\n\ncalibrate!(FlashMMA(), T; dim, n, force): measure :flash_mma on this device for (dim, T) at length n and persist it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.calibrate_mma!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.clear_calibration!","page":"API reference","text":"clear_calibration!(; disk=false)\n\nDrop cached calibrations. In-memory only by default; disk=true also removes the persisted files of both built-in kernels, which is what you want after changing a kernel, since a stale calibration would then measure something that no longer exists (a changed source tag also invalidates it).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.clear_calibration!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.tune_lengths","page":"API reference","text":"tune_lengths() -> Vector{Int}\n\nThe sequence lengths the built-in kernels are tuned for when no length is given — by tune_flash_kernel! / tune_flash_mma! and by the first device call that finds no variant. Default [256, 512], which suits most binder-design work; set it per project with set_tune_lengths! (e.g. [256, 512, 768, 1024] for larger proteins).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.tune_lengths"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.set_tune_lengths!","page":"API reference","text":"set_tune_lengths!(lengths)\n\nPersist the default tune_lengths in the active project's LocalPreferences.toml. Takes effect immediately; already tuned lengths stay registered.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.set_tune_lengths!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#LuxTriangleAttention.resolve_defaults","page":"API reference","text":"resolve_defaults(defaults, keys)\n\nUtility to resolve default values for a set of keys. This is used to handle nested  configuration for bias and other layer options.\n\nArguments\n\ndefaults: The default value(s). Can be:\nNamedTuple: contains specific overrides.\nBool: applied to all keys.\nTuple{Bool, NamedTuple}: first element is the fallback Bool, second is a NamedTuple of overrides.\nkeys: A NTuple{N, Symbol} of keys to resolve defaults for.\n\nReturns\n\nA NamedTuple containing the resolved values for all requested keys.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.resolve_defaults"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/1-layers/#1.-Layers,-masks-and-bias","page":"Layers, masks and bias","text":"The package has three layers you use directly, and two small ones they are built from:\n\nlayer what input\nTriangleAttention starting / ending node attention with a triangle bias (AlphaFold2 Algorithms 13, 14) [C, Ni, Nj, B]\nTriangleMultiplication outgoing / incoming triangle multiplication (AlphaFold2 Algorithms 11, 12) [C, N, N, B]\nAttention multi-head attention with optional gate, pair bias and mask [C, N, B], [C, N, S, B] or a tuple\nTriMulCore the projections and contraction inside TriangleMultiplication [C, N, N, B]\nGatedLinearUnit, SwiGLU gated projections [C, ...]\n\nAll of them are ordinary Lux layers: Lux.setup makes the parameters and state, and a call returns (y, st).","title":"1. Layers, masks and bias"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/1-layers/#Triangle-attention","page":"Layers, masks and bias","text":"using Lux, LuxTriangleAttention, Random\n\nrng = Random.default_rng()\nx = randn(Float32, 64, 32, 32, 1)               # [C, Ni, Nj, B]\n\nattn = TriangleAttention(64, 32, 4; is_starting=true)\nps, st = Lux.setup(rng, attn)\ny, st = attn(x, ps, st)                          # [64, 32, 32, 1]\n\nThe arguments are the input channels, the head dimension and the number of heads. With is_starting=true each (i, j) attends over its row (the starting node, AlphaFold2 Algorithm 13); with false over its column (the ending node, AlphaFold2 Algorithm 14). The ending node transposes i and j around the same computation.\n\nAlphaFold2 Algorithm 13, triangular gated self-attention around the starting node, for each head h:\n\nbeginaligned\nz_ij leftarrow mathrmLayerNorm(z_ij) \nq^h_ij k^h_ij v^h_ij = mathrmLinearNoBias(z_ij) \nb^h_ij = mathrmLinearNoBias(z_ij) \ng^h_ij = mathrmsigmoidbig(mathrmLinear(z_ij)big) \na^h_ijk = mathrmsoftmax_kBig(tfrac1sqrtc q^h_ij^top k^h_ik + b^h_jkBig) \no^h_ij = g^h_ij odot sum_k a^h_ijk v^h_ik \ntildez_ij = mathrmLinearbig(mathrmconcat_h(o^h_ij)big)\nendaligned\n\nAlphaFold2 Algorithm 14, around the ending node, differs in lines 5 and 6 only:\n\nbeginaligned\na^h_ijk = mathrmsoftmax_kBig(tfrac1sqrtc q^h_ij^top k^h_kj + b^h_kiBig) \no^h_ij = g^h_ij odot sum_k a^h_ijk v^h_kj\nendaligned\n\nHere c is the head dimension. The Linear layers carry biases unless use_bias says otherwise, and the optional mask (see Masks) is not part of the algorithms: it removes keys from the softmax.\n\nThe triangle bias is not an input: the layer projects it from the normalised x itself, as the references do, and hands it to the inner Attention with bias_layout=:qk. Keyword arguments the layer does not use itself (chunk_size, use_kernel, use_gate, ...) are forwarded to that Attention.","title":"Triangle attention"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/1-layers/#Triangle-multiplication","page":"Layers, masks and bias","text":"mul = TriangleMultiplication(64, 128; is_outgoing=true)   # channels, hidden channels\nps, st = Lux.setup(rng, mul)\ny, st = mul(x, ps, st)                                    # [64, 32, 32, 1]\n\nis_outgoing=true is AlphaFold2 Algorithm 11 (triangular multiplicative update using \"outgoing\" edges) and false is AlphaFold2 Algorithm 12 (\"incoming\" edges):\n\nbeginaligned\nz_ij leftarrow mathrmLayerNorm(z_ij) \na_ij b_ij = mathrmsigmoidbig(mathrmLinear(z_ij)big) odot mathrmLinear(z_ij) \ng_ij = mathrmsigmoidbig(mathrmLinear(z_ij)big) \ntildez_ij = g_ij odot mathrmLinearBig(mathrmLayerNormbig(textstylesum_k a_ik odot b_jkbig)Big)\nqquad text(AlphaFold2 Algorithm 11 outgoing)\nendaligned\n\nAlphaFold2 Algorithm 12 (incoming) sums over the first index instead, sum_k a_ki odot b_kj; everything else is the same. The projections run token-major (xᵀ Wᵀ) so that the contraction is a batched matrix product on contiguous [N, N] planes without a permutedims; the parameters are those of the plain Dense layers (see ChannelsLast), so weights from a PyTorch reference load the usual way.","title":"Triangle multiplication"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/1-layers/#Masks","page":"Layers, masks and bias","text":"A mask is true where the position is kept. Pass inputs as a NamedTuple with x and mask:\n\nmask = rand(Bool, 32, 32, 1)                     # [Ni, Nj, B]\ny, st = attn((; x, mask), ps, st)\ny, st = mul((; x, mask), ps, st)\n\nFor TriangleAttention and TriangleMultiplication the mask is the pair mask [Ni, Nj, B]. A bare Attention also takes a sequence mask, and prep_mask reshapes either to the internal format:\n\ngiven becomes\n[N, B] [N, 1, 1, B]\n[Ni, Nj, B] (or [N, S, B]) [Ni, 1, 1, Nj, B]\n\nmodel = Attention(64, 32, 4)                     # channels, head dimension, heads\nps, st = Lux.setup(rng, model)\nx = randn(Float32, 64, 32, 1)                    # [C, N, B]\nmask = rand(Bool, 32, 1)                         # [N, B]\ny, st = model((; x, mask), ps, st)\n\nnote: A query with no live key\nIf every key of a row is masked, the query attends uniformly and returns the mean of v, as in OpenFold and Boltz2 (whose finite 1e9·(mask - 1) bias makes all logits equal). It is never NaN, in any precision, on any path including the kernels. See SDPA.","title":"Masks"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/1-layers/#Bias-layouts","page":"Layers, masks and bias","text":"Attention takes an optional bias of shape [H, Nq, Nk, B] (the PyTorch pair-bias layout) in the bias field. prep_bias permutes it into the internal [Nk, Nq, H, 1, B] format, according to the layer's bias_layout:\n\n:qk, used by TriangleAttention. For an input [C, Ni, Nj, B] and a bias [H, Ni, Nj, B] the bias becomes [Ni, Nj, H, 1, B]. The input is permuted too (for the ending node), so the bias needs no transpose.\n:kq (the default), matching AttentionPairBias of the AlphaFold3 and Boltz references. For [C, N, S, B] and a bias [H, Nq, Nk, B] it becomes [Nk, Nq, H, 1, B], the transpose that accounts for Julia attending over dimension 3 where Python attends over dimension 4.\n\nusing Static: static\n\nx = randn(Float32, 16, 32, 32, 1)\nbias = randn(Float32, 4, 32, 32, 1)\n\nprep_bias(bias, x, static(:qk))                  # [Ni, Nj, H, 1, B]\nprep_bias(bias, x, static(:kq))                  # [Nj, Ni, H, 1, B]\n\nmodel = Attention(16, 8, 4; bias_layout=:kq)\nps, st = Lux.setup(rng, model)\ny, st = model((; x, bias), ps, st)               # prep_bias is applied for you\n\n(prep_bias takes the layout as a static symbol, which is how the layer stores it.)","title":"Bias layouts"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/1-layers/#Cross-attention-and-the-fused-projection","page":"Layers, masks and bias","text":"Pass a tuple (q_input, kv_input) or (q, k, v) for cross-attention:\n\ncross = Attention(64, 48, 48, 32, 4)             # chn_q, chn_k, chn_v, head_dim, heads\nps, st = Lux.setup(rng, cross)\nxq, xkv = randn(Float32, 64, 16, 1), randn(Float32, 48, 24, 1)\ny, st = cross((xq, xkv), ps, st)                 # [64, 16, 1]\n\nfuse_qkv=true (the default) uses one Dense for q, k and v when their channels match, and one for k and v when only those match. use_gate=false removes the sigmoid output gate, and use_bias takes a Bool or a NamedTuple over (:qkv, :q, :kv, :k, :v, :gate, :out) (see resolve_defaults).","title":"Cross-attention and the fused projection"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/1-layers/#Reducing-the-score-tensor","page":"Layers, masks and bias","text":"The plain attention path materialises the [Nq, Nk, H, Nj, B] scores, which is O(N³·H) for a pair input (about 1 GiB per op at N = 400). Two options keep that bounded, and they are alternatives (combining them throws):\n\nuse_kernel=true: a fused kernel that never materialises the scores. See Fused attention kernels.\nchunk_size=n: attend over slices of n along dimension 4. See Compiling with Reactant.","title":"Reducing the score tensor"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/1-layers/#Verifying-against-the-references","page":"Layers, masks and bias","text":"The test suite compares every layer to the PyTorch implementations of AlphaFold2, AlphaFold3 and Boltz2, across precisions, masks, bias layouts and chunk sizes. test/python/ sets up the reference environment.","title":"Verifying against the references"},{"category":"section","location":"kernel-operands/#Kernel-operands","page":"Kernel operands","text":"What an attention kernel reads, for anyone writing one or reading the built-in ones. The operands are handed over in place: nothing is permuted, padded or copied beyond what this page says.\n\nNotation: hd is the head dimension, H the heads, Nq the attention length (queries = keys), Nj the outer extent (== Nq for triangle attention, 1 for a plain [hd, H, N, B] sequence), B the batch and T the storage type (Float32, Float16 or BFloat16; BFloat16s.jl's, the type Metal.jl and Reactant use). Every array is column-major, first axis fastest.\n\noperand shape eltype notes\nq, k, v, dO, o [hd, H, Nq, Nj, B] T offset d + hd·(h + H·(j + Nq·(i + Nj·b)))\nlse, delta [1, Nq, H, Nj, B] Float32 offset j + Nq·(h + H·(i + Nj·b)); natural-log logsumexp, and Σ_d o·dO\nbias [Nk, Nq, H, B] T keys fastest; shared across Nj\ndbias [Nk, Nq, H, B] Float32 accumulate into a zeroed OutArray; sum over Nj\nmask [Nk, Nj, B] Bool (1 byte) true = DROP the key: a key mask per outer index. The layer's mask is a KEEP mask; _sdpa_prep negates it\n\nAn absent bias or mask becomes a 1-element placeholder when skip = true, which is only for a kernel whose binaries are specialised never to read it (the built-in ones). With skip = false it becomes a zero bias and an all-live mask at full size, which is what trifast takes.\n\nTwo things that are silent when wrong:\n\nBatch and head are not adjacent in the [hd, H, Nq, Nj, B] arrays or in lse and delta. A kernel that walks a combined bh = b·H + h index with one stride is right only for B = 1. The bias is (b, h)-adjacent.\nEach array and each scalar is its own Metal buffer slot. A launch binds at most 31 of them, so derive strides in the kernel rather than passing one per dimension.","title":"Kernel operands"},{"category":"section","location":"kernel-operands/#The-built-in-argument-lists","page":"Kernel operands","text":"Written once, in src/kernels/stage_args.jl (stage_args, used by the op and by the tuner alike), and pinned by test/kernels/stage_args.test.jl. Each launch's threadgroups are (cld(Nq, rows), Nj, H·B) with rows the binary's tile[1].\n\n:default   fwd  o lse | q k v bias_t mask | scale neg_inf n nouter nh mheads\n           dq   dq dbias_t | q k v bias_t mask dO lse delta | scale n nouter nh mheads\n           dk   dk | q k v bias mask dO lse delta | scale n nouter nh mheads\n           dv   dv | q k bias mask dO lse | scale n nouter nh mheads\n:flash_mma fwd  o lse | q k v bias mask | scale n nouter nh mheads\n           dkv  dk dv dbias | q k v bias mask dO lse delta | scale n nouter nh mheads\n           dq   dq | q k v bias mask dO lse delta | scale n nouter nh mheads\n\nOutputs come first, then the inputs, then the scalars: scale = 1/√hd (Float32), n = Nq, nouter = Nj, nh = H, mheads = H (all Int32). bias_t and dbias_t are the pair bias with its two pair axes swapped ([Nq, Nk, H·B], queries fastest), which a kernel with one thread per query reads coalesced.","title":"The built-in argument lists"},{"category":"section","location":"kernel-operands/#Variants","page":"Kernel operands","text":"The built-in kernels key on (dtype, dim, N, hasbias, hasmask); see Fused attention kernels.\n\nnote: Internal\nThe helper functions that build these operands (_sdpa_prep, _finish, _full, _res, ...) are internal. They are documented in their docstrings and are the quickest route to correct operands, but they may change between versions.","title":"Variants"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/4-half-precision/#4.-Half-precision","page":"Half precision","text":"Float16 tops out at 65504, and real protein activations get there. The layers handle Float16 and BFloat16 (BFloat16s.jl's type, the one Metal.jl and Reactant use) so that the result stays finite and close to a Float64 reference computed from the same rounded inputs. Casting a model is the usual Lux call:\n\nusing Lux, LuxTriangleAttention, Random\n\nattn = TriangleAttention(32, 8, 2)\nps, st = Lux.setup(Random.default_rng(), attn)\nps16, st16 = Lux.f16(ps), Lux.f16(st)\n\nx16 = Float16.(randn(Float32, 32, 64, 64, 1))\ny16, _ = attn(x16, ps16, st16)\n\nTwo reductions over long axes overflow on real data, and the package treats each:\n\nwhere why it overflows what is done\nthe variance in a LayerNorm Σx² over a few hundred channels passes 65504 at |x| ≈ 20 StableLayerNorm accumulates in Float32\nthe contraction in triangle multiplication Σₖ aₖ·bₖ over N positions (lvert Xrvert ≈ 4.8e5 at N = 494 in AlphaFold2's multimer template stack) a ContractionMode, below\nthe attention logits before the 1/√d scale 9e4 on Boltz2 activations, if the compiler reassociates the scale the chunked and kernel paths run in Float32 internally","title":"4. Half precision"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/4-half-precision/#LayerNorm","page":"Half precision","text":"StableLayerNorm is a Lux.LayerNorm with Float32 statistics for 16-bit data and exactly the wrapped layer for everything else (bitwise, so Lux's and LuxLib's specialisations for the array type still apply). Its parameters are the wrapped layer's own, so weights load unchanged. TriangleAttention and TriangleMultiplication already use it; use it in your own blocks the same way:\n\nln = StableLayerNorm((128,); dims=1)         # same parameter tree as Lux.LayerNorm((128,); dims=1)\nln0 = LayerNormNoBias((128,); dims=1)        # bias frozen at zero","title":"LayerNorm"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/4-half-precision/#Triangle-multiplication:-three-contraction-modes","page":"Half precision","text":"The contraction picks one path, statically, from the data's element type and the layer's state:\n\nmode when what runs\nFullPrecision not 16-bit data the ordinary code path\nHalfSafe 16-bit data, ordinary parameters (e.g. a plain Lux.f16(ps)) the contraction in Float32: always correct, slower and wider\nFolded(k) the projections were pre-scaled by 2⁻ᵏ the contraction sums roughly a mean, so it stays in range in Float16; fast and exact\n\nWithout doing anything, 16-bit data takes HalfSafe. To get Folded, scale the parameters once with fold_trimul, before casting to 16 bits, for the longest sequence they must serve (fold_k):\n\nusing LuxTriangleAttention: fold_trimul, fold_k    # public, not exported\n\nmul = TriangleMultiplication(128, 128)\nps, st = Lux.setup(Random.default_rng(), mul)\n\nps, st = fold_trimul(ps, st, fold_k(1024))   # 4ᵏ ≥ 1024  →  k = 5\nps16, st16 = Lux.f16(ps), Lux.f16(st)\n\ny16, _ = mul(x16, ps16, st16)\n\nfold_k(L) is the smallest k with 4ᵏ ≥ L. Folding multiplies each of the two value projections by 2⁻ᵏ, so the contraction is divided by 4ᵏ, and the output LayerNorm (scale-invariant, with its ε scaled by 16⁻ᵏ) removes it exactly. A larger k than needed only makes values smaller, which Float16 represents at full relative precision down to 6e-5, so L is a bound on the sequence length, not the length of any particular input.\n\nThe mode is decided by the state: st.core.fold_k exists exactly when the weights carry the 2⁻ᵏ, whatever the element type. A layer can therefore never apply a 4ᵏ its weights do not carry, nor miss one they do. An Int in a Lux state is a compile-time constant under Reactant, so the choice is free at runtime. contraction_mode(x, st.core) (see contraction_mode) shows what a call will use.","title":"Triangle multiplication: three contraction modes"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/4-half-precision/#Attention","page":"Half precision","text":"Masks are applied by selecting mask_value(T) (-floatmax(T)/2, finite in every precision), never by adding -Inf or 1e9·(mask - 1). The latter is -Inf in Float16 (Float16(-1e9) == -Inf), which turned a fully padded window into NaN. A row with no live key attends uniformly everywhere (see SDPA).\nChunked attention is Float16-safe on the CPU and, rolled, under Reactant (a chunk runs in Float32 there). The plain use_kernel=false, chunk_size=nothing path is LuxLib's attention, which is not Float16-safe under Reactant and materialises every score; use chunk_size or use_kernel for 16-bit data in production.\nFused kernels accumulate in Float32 whatever the storage type. A bias in another precision is converted with saturation at ±floatmax(T)/2 rather than overflowing to ±Inf.\n\nwarning: Peaked attention in the backward pass\nThe kernels take Float16 operands for their matrix products, the FlashAttention-2 convention. A loss-scaled dO large enough to push dS = P·(dP − δ) past 65504 gives non-finite gradients, as it would there. Whether that happens depends on the inputs (peaked attention together with a large aligned |v|·|dO|), not on the configuration, and it is not detectable by the tuner. On random problems dS stays far from overflow even at dO ≈ 2e4.","title":"Attention"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/4-half-precision/#Precision-utilities","page":"Half precision","text":"These are public but not exported (LuxTriangleAttention.ishalf, ...). ishalf(T) says whether T is a 16-bit float type, mask_value gives the finite masking value for a type or array, and layernorm_f32stats is the one-pass Float32-statistics normalisation behind StableLayerNorm, for use in your own layers.","title":"Precision utilities"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/5-custom-kernels/#5.-Your-own-attention-kernel","page":"Your own attention kernel","text":"You have compiled triangle-attention kernels, a .metallib or a .cubin/.ptx, with a forward and a backward, possibly spread over several files. This is how to make TriangleAttention(...; use_kernel=true) run yours instead of the built-in ones.\n\nThe machinery is KernelDispatch.jl, whose documentation explains the concepts used here (ops, kernels, variants, call_binary). In short: attention is the op SDPA, a kernel is a type plus a few methods, and KernelDispatch passes the arguments you give it straight to your binary, in the order you choose, in plain Julia.\n\nusing LuxTriangleAttention\nuse_kernel!(:trifast)                                          # the next top-level statement on\nKernelDispatch.forward(SDPA(), Trifast(), q, k, v, mask, bias) # or one call with it, no global switch","title":"5. Your own attention kernel"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/5-custom-kernels/#1.-A-worked-example:-trifast","page":"Your own attention kernel","text":"test/support/trifast_kernel.jl defines trifast's kernel and test/bench/kernels/registry_setup.jl registers its four Triton metallibs. It has a forward, then a backward split three ways: dq (which also writes delta), then dkv, then a dedicated dbias. Abridged:\n\nimport KernelDispatch\nusing KernelDispatch: KernelBinary, OutArray, call_binary\n\nstruct Trifast <: KernelDispatch.AbstractKernel end\nKernelDispatch.kernelname(::Trifast) = :trifast\n\nfunction KernelDispatch.forward(op::SDPA, k::Trifast, q0, k0, v0, mask, bias)\n    q, kk, v, m, b, d = LuxTriangleAttention._sdpa_prep(q0, k0, v0, mask, bias, false)\n    o, lse = call_binary(op, k, :fwd,\n        OutArray(d.T, size(q)), OutArray(Float32, 1, d.Nq, d.H, d.Nj, d.B),    # outputs\n        q, kk, v, b, m, scale, neg_inf, Int32(d.Nq), Int32(d.H);               # inputs, then scalars\n        key=(nameof(d.T), d.hd), extent=(d.Nq, d.Nq, d.H * d.B))               # which variant; the problem\n    return LuxTriangleAttention._finish(o, lse, q0)\nend\n\nKernelDispatch.backward(op::SDPA, k::Trifast, (q0, k0, v0, mask, bias), (o, lse), (dO, _)) = ...\n#   three `call_binary`s: (:dq → delta, dq), (:dkv → dk, dv), (:dbias → dbias, zero-filled);\n#   returns (dq, dk, dv, nothing, dbias) in the arguments' shapes and element types\n\nKernelDispatch.register_kernel!(SDPA(), Trifast())\nKernelDispatch.add_variant!(SDPA(), Trifast(), (:Float32, 32);                 # variant key: (dtype, dim)\n    fwd=KernelBinary(\"_fwd.metallib\"; threadgroup=1024, tile=(32, 1, 1)),      # 32 query rows per group\n    dq=KernelBinary(\"_bwd_q.metallib\"; threadgroup=1024, tile=(32, 1, 1)),\n    dkv=KernelBinary(\"_bwd_kv.metallib\"; threadgroup=1024, tile=(32, 1, 1)),\n    dbias=KernelBinary(\"_bwd_b.metallib\"; threadgroup=1024, tile=(32, 32, 1))) # 32×32 pair blocks\n\nKernelBinarys are copied into the kernel cache and recorded in a manifest, so registering is a one-time step and survives a restart. The cache is KernelDispatch': ~/.cache/KernelDispatch/ops/LuxTriangleAttention.sdpa/<kernel>/ (KERNELDISPATCH_CACHE moves the root).\nThe entry point looked up in each binary is the file's basename, unless you pass entry=.\ncall_binary binds its arguments in the order given: each OutArray(T, dims...) is a fresh output (zero=true zero-fills it, for a kernel that accumulates) and is returned, in order; each array is bound in place; each number is bound by value.\nLaunch size. call_binary launches cld.(extent, tile) threadgroups: the call's extent (the problem size, here (Nq, Nq, H·B)) over each binary's tile (how much one threadgroup covers, a property of the build). Trifast thus gets (n/32, n, H·B) and, for :dbias, (n/32, n/32, H·B). There is no grid method to write.\nUnder Reactant with a Metal client the same call becomes a custom call inside the compiled program.\n\nLuxTriangleAttention._sdpa_prep and _finish are internal helpers that turn the layer's operands into the form every built-in kernel reads, and back. They are the easiest way to get the operands right, but they are not API: check them against your version. What they produce is on the Kernel operands page.","title":"1. A worked example: trifast"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/5-custom-kernels/#2.-Declare-the-types","page":"Your own attention kernel","text":"A binary takes raw pointers, so a Float16 array handed to a kernel compiled for float* would run and compute something else. KernelDispatch therefore checks the types of a launch against the signature the binary was built for and refuses a mismatch, naming the argument. A prebuilt binary declares it:\n\nF, M = Vector{Float32}, Vector{Bool}\nKernelBinary(\"_fwd.metallib\"; threadgroup=1024, tile=(32, 1, 1),\n    signature=(F, F, F, F, F, F, M, Float32, Float32, Int32, Int32))   # o lse q k v bias mask | scale neg_inf n h\n\nOne entry per bound argument, outputs included, in order: Vector{T} for any array, the exact type for a scalar (Int32(n), not n). Without one the first launch warns once and prints the signature= that fits. The order of two arguments of the same type is not checked, which is what the next section is for.\n\nFloat32, Float16 and BFloat16 buffers are supported, and one launch may mix them (a Float16 o beside a Float32 lse). The built-in kernels take q, k, v, dO and the bias in the storage type (the op converts a k, v, dO or bias of another precision to q's) and keep lse, delta and dbias in Float32.","title":"2. Declare the types"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/5-custom-kernels/#3.-Pin-your-argument-lists","page":"Your own attention kernel","text":"Nothing checks that the argument order matches your binary: a mis-ordered list binds without error and computes garbage. Record what your forward and backward bind with KernelDispatch.record_bindings (each record has a label, the slots and the threadgroups groups) and compare it in a test, slots and grids; a mis-ordered list also shows up as results that differ from a reference implementation.","title":"3. Pin your argument lists"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/5-custom-kernels/#4.-Variants","page":"Your own attention kernel","text":"variant_key (or call_binary(...; key=...)) says which build serves a call. A third-party kernel compiled once for every length keys on (dtype, dim), like trifast. The built-in kernels key on (dtype, dim, N, hasbias, hasmask): a call by its own length N, a variant by the length it was tuned for, with nearest picking the closest tuned length (absolute distance, ties to the larger) but never a different dtype, dimension or presence. Their build! tunes the default lengths lazily when there is no variant at all. See Fused attention kernels.","title":"4. Variants"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/5-custom-kernels/#5.-Differentiation","page":"Your own attention kernel","text":"backward(op, kernel, args, outs, cotangents) is what makes a kernel differentiable. Two things call it:\n\nEnzyme, eagerly on device arrays, through KernelDispatch' one rule on forward. Enzyme + Metal is not a training stack: the rule's gradients are exact, but Enzyme cannot differentiate the ordinary Metal.jl code around it. Its payoff is CUDA.\nthe layer-level reverse helper, eager or traced: on a trace it emits the backward custom calls.\n\nA gradient traced through a kernel under Reactant throws until Enzyme-JAX can differentiate a custom call (EnzymeAD/Enzyme#2516). Use use_kernel=false for differentiated traced programs.","title":"5. Differentiation"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/5-custom-kernels/#6.-Other-backends-and-operations","page":"Your own attention kernel","text":"A prebuilt binary belongs to one backend, so a kernel that must run on both Metal and CUDA ships a binary for each, or is written in KernelAbstractions and compiled with KernelDispatch.ka_compile for whichever backend is loaded. On CUDA, a .cubin (built for one architecture, which target records) or a portable .ptx takes the place of the .metallib:\n\nKernelBinary(\"fwd.cubin\"; entry=\"attn_fwd\", threadgroup=128, tile=(128, 1, 1), target=\"sm_86\")\n\nAnything beyond attention gets its own KernelDispatch op the same way: an AbstractKernelOp, its kernels, and their forward and backward. See the KernelDispatch tutorials.\n\nNot supported: .so / torch-extension kernels (DeepSpeed4Science, cuEquivariance) ship no raw binary with a documented argument list, so they need a C-call mechanism that does not exist here.","title":"6. Other backends and operations"},{"category":"section","location":"internals/#Internals","page":"Internals","text":"The docstrings of everything that is neither exported nor public: the attention routing, the tuner, the stage launcher and the kernel sources. Documented for people reading or extending the package, and not covered by any compatibility promise. The Kernel operands page explains the data these functions move around.","title":"Internals"},{"category":"constant","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.DO_SCALE","page":"Internals","text":"DO_SCALE\n\nThe large-dO sanity check of the backward stages: the verification problem again with dO (and so delta and every gradient) scaled by 256, a power of two, exact in every storage type; its largest dO entry is then ~1e3. The errors are relative, so the scale cancels.\n\nWhat it does NOT decide: whether a Float16 dS = P·(dP − δ) can overflow is a property of the INPUTS — it needs peaked attention (P near 1/2…1) together with a large aligned |v|·|dO| (|dP| ≤ dim · max|v| · max|dO|) — not of the configuration. On this random problem P is spread over ~67 keys and Float16 dS stays far from overflow even at dO ~ 2e4 (measured on an M1 Pro). Float16 operands are the FlashAttention-2 convention and carry its caveat: a loss-scaled dO large enough to push dS past 65504 gives non-finite gradients, as it would there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.DO_SCALE"},{"category":"constant","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.FLASH_MMA_NAME","page":"Internals","text":"FLASH_MMA_NAME\n\nThe registry name of the matrix-unit kernel. Like :default it is built by this package (tune_flash_mma!), never registered from user files.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.FLASH_MMA_NAME"},{"category":"constant","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.FLASH_MMA_STAGES","page":"Internals","text":"The stages of :flash_mma, in launch order: the forward, then :dkv and :dq.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.FLASH_MMA_STAGES"},{"category":"constant","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.FLASH_STAGES","page":"Internals","text":"FLASH_STAGES\n\nThe stages of the built-in kernel, in launch order: the forward, then the three backward passes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.FLASH_STAGES"},{"category":"constant","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.FULL_SWEEP_MAX","page":"Internals","text":"The largest grid swept in full; larger ones are searched by coordinate descent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.FULL_SWEEP_MAX"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.DeviceCaps","page":"Internals","text":"DeviceCaps\n\nWhat a backend reports for the candidate configurations to be built. Deliberately small: anything that can be measured is measured rather than declared.\n\nFields\n\nwarp: threads per warp / wavefront / SIMD group. Group sizes are multiples of this.\nmax_threads: the device's ceiling on threads per group. A NECESSARY but not sufficient bound — a compiled pipeline's own limit (KernelDispatch.max_threads) is checked per candidate.\nmax_shmem: bytes of local memory available to one group.\nname: device identifier, used as the calibration key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.DeviceCaps"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA","page":"Internals","text":"FlashMMA\n\n:flash_mma: the flash-attention kernel on the GPU's MATRIX units, built by tune_flash_mma!.\n\nMMA is matrix multiply-accumulate: the hardware operation C += A·B on a small tile (8×8 simdgroup_matrix on Metal; tensor cores on NVIDIA). A simdgroup owns a block of query rows and computes Q·Kᵀ and P·V (and the backward products) a fragment at a time; only the row-wise softmax reductions run on the lanes that hold the fragments. The fragment operations are per-backend hooks (flash_mma/), Metal's in the extension. Contrast FlashScalar, which uses plain per-thread arithmetic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA"},{"category":"type","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar","page":"Internals","text":"FlashScalar\n\n:default: this package's flash-attention kernel with SCALAR arithmetic, built by tune_flash_kernel!.\n\nEach thread owns one query. A threadgroup stages a tile of keys and values in local memory and every thread walks it with ordinary fused multiply-adds, keeping its running softmax state and output row in registers; the work is spread over queries, never over the matrix units. Contrast FlashMMA, which computes the same algorithm on the GPU's matrix units. Both are written in KernelAbstractions; what differs is how the products are computed. The tuned parameters are the threadgroup size tg, the key-tile size kt and the sub-block size sb (flash/).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#KernelDispatch.nearest-Tuple{SDPA, Union{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA, LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar}, Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"nearest(::SDPA, ::BuiltinKernel, keys, key)\n\nThe variant for a call with no exact one: same dtype, head dim and bias/mask presence (a different ABI or binary is never substituted), then the tuned length closest to the call's, ties to the larger. Every variant computes every length; the length only decides which tuning fits best.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"KernelDispatch.nearest"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.__init__-Tuple{}","page":"Internals","text":"__init__()\n\nRegister the two attention kernels this package builds itself (:default and :flash_mma) with KernelDispatch, which loads their tuned variants from the cache — dropping any built from other kernel sources. A missing variant is tuned on demand. Which one runs is SDPA's @default_kernel (the first, or the package preference kernel.sdpa), switched for a session with use_kernel!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.__init__"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._ab_planes-Tuple{TriMulCore{Static.True}, Vararg{Any, 6}}","page":"Internals","text":"_ab_planes(m::TriMulCore, x_tok, mask, ps, st, N, B) -> (a, b, st_ab)\n\nProject the normalised input into the gated, masked a and b representations, laid out as [N, N, B·H] planes (batch slot b + B·(h-1)), ready for the batched contraction. st_ab holds the updated states of the GLU(s) that ran, keyed by their field names.\n\nWith fused = true one GLU produces both ([N²·B, 2H], a in the first H columns); the planes are contiguous views of it. With fused = false, glu_a and glu_b produce one each.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._ab_planes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._apply_tri_mask-Tuple{Any, Nothing}","page":"Internals","text":"_apply_tri_mask(ab, mask)\n\nZero the rows of token-major ab::[N²·B, ·] whose pair is masked out (true = keep). mask is [N, N, B], or [N, N] shared by every batch element. Out of place, so it is differentiable; under Reactant it fuses into the GLU broadcast.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._apply_tri_mask"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._atomic_fadd!","page":"Internals","text":"_atomic_fadd!(ptr, idx, val)\n\nAtomically add val into element idx (1-based, the same convention unsafe_load/unsafe_store! use throughout this file) of the Float32 buffer ptr points to.\n\nDeclared here with no implementation. An atomic add has no portable expression over KernelAbstractions's raw-pointer kernels (see note 2 in forward.jl for why raw pointers are used at all), so a backend extension supplies the actual instruction for its own pointer type, e.g. the Metal extension adds a method for LLVMPtr{Float32,AS.Device} built on Metal.atomic_fetch_add_explicit: ordinary dispatch on a function this package owns, like the backend hooks in device.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._atomic_fadd!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._chunked_sdpa-Tuple{Nothing, Vararg{Any, 5}}","page":"Internals","text":"_chunked_sdpa(chunk_size, q, k, v, mask, bias)\n\nScaled dot-product attention, optionally split into chunk_size slices along dim 4 of q — the first axis the attention batches over, since dim 3 is the token axis being attended.\n\nFor a pair input [C, Ni, Nj, B] dim 4 is Nj (a TOKEN axis, not the sample batch), and the scores are [Nq, Nk, H, Nj, B] = O(N³·H) — ~1 GiB per op at N=400. Splitting it bounds the live score tensor to chunk_size · N² · H with no change to the result, and does so at B == 1. Mirrors chunk_size_tri_attn in the openfold/boltz references.\n\nOperands are sliced only when they actually span the chunked axis (size(·, 4) == L); a broadcast axis of size 1 — which is what prep_bias produces for the :qk layout — is passed through whole.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._chunked_sdpa"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._chunked_sdpa_unrolled-Tuple{Int64, Vararg{Any, 5}}","page":"Internals","text":"_chunked_sdpa_unrolled(chunk_size, q, k, v, mask, bias)\n\nChunked attention as an ordinary Julia loop. Executed sequentially it bounds the live score tensor, which is the intended win on the native path — measured at N=512 on one PairformerLayer: 3.2 GB less peak for ~12% more time.\n\nUnder Reactant.@compile this loop is UNROLLED into the graph, and because the chunks are mutually independent XLA is free to schedule them concurrently and keep every slice live, completely negating  any memory benefits. The Reactant extension therefore overrides _chunked_sdpa with a rolled @trace  loop; this method stays the native path and the fallback for chunk sizes the rolled form cannot take.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._chunked_sdpa_unrolled"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._kaccum-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{NTuple{D, Float32}, Any, Any, Any}} where D","page":"Internals","text":"accv .+ w .* ks[:, c] — a plain accumulate, no rescale (the backward carries no running max).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._kaccum"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._kbias-Tuple{Any, Any, Val{true}}","page":"Internals","text":"_kbias(bias, idx, Val(HB)) -> Float32\n\nOne bias value, base 2 (* log2e), or 0 without reading bias when HB = false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._kbias"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._kdot-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{NTuple{D, Float32}, Any, Any}} where D","page":"Internals","text":"_kdot(qv, ks, c) -> Float32\n\nInner product of a register-resident vector against column c of a staged tile, fully unrolled.\n\nAlso the reason every helper here takes its pointers and offsets as PARAMETERS: a closure that captures a kernel argument loses its type under @kernel — inference gives up and compilation fails with \"unsupported call to jlmulfloat\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._kdot"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._kexp2-Union{Tuple{SB}, Tuple{NTuple{SB, Float32}, Any}} where SB","page":"Internals","text":"exp2.(s .- mn), straight-line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._kexp2"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._kload-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{Any, Any, Val{D}}} where D","page":"Internals","text":"_kload(q, qb, Val(D)) -> NTuple{D,Float32}\n\nLoad one vector into registers, widening to the compute precision. Takes the pointer and offset as PARAMETERS for the reason given in _kdot.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._kload"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._kscale-Union{Tuple{D}, Tuple{NTuple{D, Float32}, Any}} where D","page":"Internals","text":"accv .* w\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._kscale"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._kscores-Union{Tuple{HB}, Tuple{SB}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{NTuple{D, Float32}, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Val{SB}, Val{HB}}} where {D, SB, HB}","page":"Internals","text":"_kscores(qv, ks, bias_t, btb, t, c0, ms, n, Val(SB), Val(HB)) -> NTuple{SB,Float32}\n\nBase-2 scores of the SB keys c0+1 … c0+SB of the staged tile: q·k (with scale·log2e already in q) plus, when HB, the transposed bias times log2e, plus the staged additive mask. A key past n is clamped to a valid bias address; its mask term already kills it. With HB = false the bias term is not generated at all, so bias_t is never read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._kscores"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._kstage_mask!-Union{Tuple{HM}, Tuple{KT}, Tuple{TG}, Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Val{TG}, Val{KT}, Val{HM}}} where {TG, KT, HM}","page":"Internals","text":"_kstage_mask!(ms, mask, mb, t, n, tid, dead_value, Val(TG), Val(KT), Val(HM))\n\nStage one key tile's mask into local memory as an additive term: 0 for a live key, dead_value for a masked key or one past n. Strided by TG, so it is correct for KT > TG too. With HM = false only the keys past n are dead and mask is never read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._kstage_mask!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._kstage_rows!-Union{Tuple{KT}, Tuple{TG}, Tuple{DIM}, Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Val{T}, Val{DIM}, Val{TG}, Val{KT}}} where {T, DIM, TG, KT}","page":"Internals","text":"_kstage_rows!(qs, dos, q, dO, qb0, hstep, t, n, tid, Val(T), Val(DIM), Val(TG), Val(KT))\n\nStage one tile of KT query rows of q and dO into local memory, for the key-side kernels. Rows past n are zero-filled; their staged lse (see _kstage_stats!) kills them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._kstage_rows!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._kstage_stats!-Union{Tuple{KT}, Tuple{TG}, Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Val{TG}, Val{KT}}} where {TG, KT}","page":"Internals","text":"_kstage_stats!(lses, deltas, lse, delta, lbase, t, n, tid, Val(TG), Val(KT))\n\nStage one query tile's lse (converted to base 2) and, unless deltas === nothing, delta. A query past n gets lse = +floatmax, so its p = exp2(s - floatmax) is exactly zero.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._kstage_stats!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._kstore!-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{Any, NTuple{D, Float32}, Any, Any, Type{T}}} where {D, T}","page":"Internals","text":"_kstore!(o, accv, w, qb, T)\n\nStore accv .* w, narrowed to the storage type. w = inv(l) in the forward, 1 or 0 in the backward.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._kstore!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._kupdate-Union{Tuple{SB}, Tuple{D}, Tuple{NTuple{D, Float32}, Any, NTuple{SB, Float32}, Any, Any}} where {D, SB}","page":"Internals","text":"_kupdate(accv, corr, p, vs, c0) -> NTuple{D,Float32}\n\nThe online-softmax accumulator update for one sub-block: accv .* corr .+ Σ_u p[u] .* vs[:, c0+u], one rescale for all SB keys.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._kupdate"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._mma","page":"Internals","text":"_mma(be, a, b, c) -> c + a·b, Float32 accumulator c; a, b both Float32, Float16 or BFloat16.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._mma"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._mma_lane","page":"Internals","text":"_mma_lane(be, lane) -> (row, col): the (0-based) entries (row + 8(s-1), col), (row + 8(s-1), col+1) that lane lane (0-based) holds in every accumulator, for each row slot s.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._mma_lane"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._mma_load_a","page":"Internals","text":"_mma_load_a(be, a, r, c) -> fragment\n_mma_load_b(be, a, r, c) -> fragment\n\nThe A operand (FM×FK block) or the B operand (FK×FN block) of local-memory matrix a whose top-left entry is a[r, c] (1-based). a is a column-major @localmem array holding the matrix as it enters the product (rows first), so a B block is read down its columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._mma_load_a"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._mma_pair","page":"Internals","text":"_mma_pair(be, f, s) -> (x, y): this lane's two entries of row slot s of fragment f, as Float32.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._mma_pair"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._mma_pfrag-Union{Tuple{E}, Tuple{Any, Type{E}, Any}} where E","page":"Internals","text":"_mma_pfrag(be, E, p1, p2, …) -> operand\n\nThe next product's A operand, of element type E, built from accumulator entries already in registers: the pairs pi = (x, y) of one row slot of one FN-wide block, listed block by block, slot by slot (FK÷FN blocks of FM÷8 slots). Falls back to a single pair set into a zero fragment, which is the case when the operand is one block of one slot (Metal).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._mma_pfrag"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._mma_rowmax","page":"Internals","text":"_mma_rowmax(be, x), _mma_rowsum(be, x): reduce x across the lanes holding one row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._mma_rowmax"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._mma_setpair","page":"Internals","text":"_mma_setpair(be, f, s, x, y) -> fragment: f with this lane's two entries of row slot s replaced.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._mma_setpair"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._mma_shape-Tuple{KernelDispatch.KernelBackend}","page":"Internals","text":"_mma_shape(be) -> (FM, FN, FK)\n\nThe product C[FM×FN] += A[FM×FK]·B[FK×FN] the backend's matrix unit computes; the kernels are written against it. FM is a multiple of 8 (FM÷8 row slots per lane, see \"The fragment shape\" in this file); FN, FK divide the head dimension (FK) and the key / query tile (FK). Defaults to the 8×8×8 of [MetalBackendTag] for any backend (the host side asks it too, with no device loaded).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._mma_shape"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._mma_zero","page":"Internals","text":"_mma_zero(be, E): a fragment of zeros of element type E, valid as an accumulator.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._mma_zero"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._sdpa-Tuple{Static.False, Int64, Vararg{Any, 5}}","page":"Internals","text":"_sdpa(use_kernel, chunk_size, q, k, v, mask, bias)\n\nEntry point from the layer forward: pick how the score tensor is kept small, by the types of the two knobs rather than by branching on their values.\n\nuse_kernel chunk_size route\nFalse nothing _sdpa — Lux.scaled_dot_product_attention\nFalse Int _chunked_sdpa — slice dim 4\nTrue nothing SDPA()(…) — a fused kernel for this device\nTrue Int error: the two are alternatives, not a combination\n\nEvery route here returns just the bare output array: Attention's forward reshapes and gates it directly. SDPA()(…) itself always returns an (output, lse) PAIR — lse is the residual a device kernel's reverse rule needs, and nothing on the host path, where there is no reverse rule to feed — so the use_kernel=true route below unwraps it with a plain first, unconditionally, rather than testing the shape of the result. See SDPA's docstring for the full routing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._sdpa"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._sdpa_prep-Tuple{Any, Any, Any, Any, Any, Bool}","page":"Internals","text":"_sdpa_prep(q, k, v, mask, bias, skip) -> (q, k, v, mask, bias, d)\n\nThe caller's operands as every attention binary reads them: rank-5 q/k/v and a [Nq, Nq, H, B] bias, all in the storage type (q's: a k, v or bias of another precision is converted, see _store and _to_storage), and a DROP mask [Nq, Nj, B] (the caller's is a KEEP mask). An absent bias or mask is a 1-element placeholder when skip (the kernel has a specialisation that never reads it), else a zero bias (adds nothing to any score) and an all-live mask. d holds the extents and flags.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._sdpa_prep"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._seq5-Union{Tuple{AbstractArray{T, 4}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"_seq5(x)\n\nA plain sequence's [hd, H, N, B] operand as the rank-5 [hd, H, N, 1, B] every kernel speaks: attention over N batched over an outer extent of ONE. A pure reshape — the memory order is identical.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._seq5"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._warn_chunking_on_gpu-Tuple{KernelDispatch.KernelBackend}","page":"Internals","text":"_warn_chunking_on_gpu(backend)\n\nSay once, on a device backend, that chunking is the wrong tool here.\n\nchunk_size exists because the host path materialises the whole [Nq, Nk, H, Nj, B] score tensor and has no other way to bound it. A fused kernel tiles the keys internally and never materialises it at all, so on a GPU chunking pays the memory cost it was meant to avoid AND serialises work the device would otherwise run at once. Reaching here with device arrays almost always means use_kernel was left at its default rather than that chunking was wanted.\n\nUses backend_of rather than a GPUArrays dependency: the trait already distinguishes MtlArray, CuArray and ROCArray, and it additionally covers a traced array on a GPU client, which an array type check would miss entirely.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._warn_chunking_on_gpu"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention._with_dead_rows-NTuple{6, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"_with_dead_rows(sdpa, q, k, v, mask, bias)\n\nsdpa(q, k, v, mask, bias) with the behaviour of a FULLY MASKED key set pinned down. Masked logits are -Inf in LuxLib, so a query whose keys are all masked softmaxes over -Inf and comes out NaN. OpenFold and Boltz2 add a large FINITE negative (1e9·(mask - 1)) instead, which makes every logit of such a row equal, so the row attends uniformly and returns the MEAN of v; this does the same, exactly and in any precision: the output of a dead (outer index, batch) slice is the mean of v over the keys, and its gradient reaches v (dO / n per key) but not q, k or bias, the logits having been replaced. The fused kernels follow the same rule, see SDPA.\n\nDone around sdpa rather than inside it: the dead slices are given an all-live mask, so sdpa stays finite there (its NaN would otherwise poison the gradient through the ifelse: 0 * NaN), and their output is then replaced. mask is [n_keys, 1, 1, …] (true = keep), so a slice is dead when any(mask; dims=1) is false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention._with_dead_rows"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.admissible-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA, Symbol, Integer, Type{T}, LuxTriangleAttention.DeviceCaps, Any}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"admissible(::FlashMMA, stage, dim, T, caps, p): within the device's thread cap and local memory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.admissible"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.admissible-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar, Symbol, Integer, Type{T}, LuxTriangleAttention.DeviceCaps, Any}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"admissible(::FlashScalar, stage, dim, T, caps, p)\n\nWhat is decidable without compiling: the staged tiles fit in local memory, and sb divides kt (the forward walks each tile in whole sub-blocks). Whether the COMPILED pipeline admits tg threads — register pressure drives that well below caps.max_threads: for the forward at dim=32 on an M1 Pro, 384 against 1024 — is checked per candidate, since answering it means compiling. T changes the space: the tiles are held in storage precision, so a narrower T legalises larger kt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.admissible"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.build_stage-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Union{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA, LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar}, Symbol, Type{T}, Integer, NamedTuple}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"build_stage(kernel, stage, T, dim, p; hasbias=true, hasmask=true) -> KernelDispatch.KernelBinary\n\nCompile one stage of a built-in kernel at the parameter point p (a NamedTuple of its tuned parameters) for the loaded device backend, or return the binary already built this session. Its tile is (stage_rows(kernel, p), 1, 1), so a launch of extent = (n, nouter, heads) dispatches (cld(n, rows), nouter, heads) groups. hasbias = false / hasmask = false select the specialisation that never reads the bias / mask (the kernels' HB/HM flags).\n\nT, dim and the parameters are compile-time constants, so each is a separate compilation (the tile loops unroll against them); n and the head count are runtime arguments, so one binary covers every length. Cold compilation is 0.2–0.9 s. The binary records its signature (the stage's kernel_argtypes / mma_argtypes without the Vals) and ka_compile names the file after those types, so a Float16 build is never served for a Float32 request, and a launch that binds other types than it was built for is refused rather than computing garbage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.build_stage"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.calibration_path-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Union{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA, LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar}, AbstractString, Integer, Type{T}, Integer}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"calibration_path(kernel, device, dim, T, n) -> String\n\nWhere a calibration is persisted: beside the kernel's registered binaries, <KernelDispatch.cache_dir(SDPA())>/<kernelname>/calibration/<device>__d<dim>__<T>__n<n>.toml. TOML, so it can be read, diffed and hand-edited.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.calibration_path"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.choose_config","page":"Internals","text":"choose_config(t) -> NamedTuple\n\nOne stage's configuration from its calibration at a length (calibrate!(…)[stage]): the fastest measured point, or, given a predicate usable(point), the fastest one it accepts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.choose_config"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.device_caps-Tuple{KernelDispatch.KernelBackend}","page":"Internals","text":"device_caps(backend) -> DeviceCaps\n\nThe device's limits. Implemented by the backend extension for its own tag.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.device_caps"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.ending_permute-Tuple{Nothing}","page":"Internals","text":"ending_permute(x)\n\nUtility to swap the i and j dimensions (2nd and 3rd spatial dimensions)  of the input tensor.\n\nArguments\n\nx: Input tensor.\n\nReturns\n\nTensor with swapped i and j dimensions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.ending_permute"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.flash_bwd_delta-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{AbstractArray{T, 4}, Any}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"flash_bwd_delta(o, dO) -> Array{Float32}\n\ndelta[j,i,bh] = Σ_d o[d,j,i,bh] * dO[d,j,i,bh], shape matching lse, the row constant both backward kernels need (Σ_j p_{j,kk}(dP_{j,kk} - delta_j) = 0 is the softmax-Jacobian identity that makes dS = p * (dP - delta) correct; see e.g. the FlashAttention-2 paper).\n\nOrdinary broadcasting, not a @kernel: the RESULT costs nothing extra to materialise (it is exactly the shape of o/dO reduced over one axis already the same size as lse), so a raw-pointer kernel buys nothing here. Works on any array backend — CPU, MtlArray, whatever q/k/v already are, for the same reason SDPA()'s CPU fallback needs no device-specific kernel of its own either, see src/kernels/sdpa_op.jl.\n\nWritten as a FUSED reduction rather than the obvious sum(Float32.(o) .* Float32.(dO); dims=1), because that form materialises the PRODUCT in full before reducing it away: one extra [dim, n, n, bh] buffer, 134 MB at N=512. Fusing is also ~3.4x faster, since the temporary is never written and read back.\n\nTwo details that are easy to get wrong:\n\nThe broadcast must be instantiated. mapreducedim! calls check_reducedims, which asks the source for axes(A, i), and an un-instantiated Broadcasted has no axes yet.\nThe multiply is done in Float32 explicitly, via the lambda, rather than by broadcasting Float32 over each operand first. For T = Float32 these agree, but for a narrower storage type broadcasted(*, o, dO) would multiply in T and only widen on the way into the accumulator, silently losing the precision the file header requires (see note 3 in forward.jl). Both operands are widened BEFORE the multiply, exactly as the unfused version did. Still lazy, so this costs no memory.\n\nBase.mapreducedim! accepts a Broadcasted source on CPU and GPU alike (Base's own AbstractArrayOrBroadcasted), so the fusion does not cost portability.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.flash_bwd_delta"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.flash_bwd_dk!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"flash_bwd_dk!(dk, q, k, v, bias, mask, dO, lse, delta,\n    scale, n, nouter, nh, mheads, Val(T), Val(DIM), Val(TG), Val(KT), Val(HB), Val(HM))\n\nBackward pass, dK, one thread per KEY, streaming query tiles. The transposed geometry of flash_bwd_dq!: q/dO (plus lse/delta) are staged into local memory instead of k/v, and the thread-resident registers hold this key's own k/v instead of q. bias is the ordinary keys-fastest layout, which is the coalesced one for a thread per key.\n\ndK[:, kk, i, bh] is written once, by the thread that owns kk, no atomics, same reasoning as dQ above. Does NOT touch dBias; see the file header.\n\nHolds kv, vv and the dkv accumulator, 3*DIM registers, the same footprint as flash_bwd_dq!. vv is resident here, and absent from flash_bwd_dv!, because dK alone needs dp = v·dO in order to form ds. That asymmetry is the entire reason the two are separate kernels rather than one: see the register-pressure note in the file header.\n\nA masked key contributes nothing at any query of a slice that has a live key (in the forward, p = 0 there for every column; a slice with none attended uniformly, and dV gives every key its share, see flash_bwd_dv!), so dK is written as ZERO for it — selected at the store, not multiplied in, so a non-finite accumulator (a fully-masked row's lse = -Inf) cannot leak through as 0 * Inf = NaN — and never by an early return, since every thread in a group must still take part in the cooperative staging and its @synchronize calls regardless of its own mask value. A query past n in a ragged tile has its staged lse set to +floatmax, which makes its p exactly zero: no branch in the loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.flash_bwd_dk!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.flash_bwd_dq!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"flash_bwd_dq!(dq, dbias_t, q, k, v, bias_t, mask, dO, lse, delta,\n    scale, n, nouter, nh, mheads, Val(T), Val(DIM), Val(TG), Val(KT), Val(HB), Val(HM))\n\nBackward pass, dQ/dBias half: one thread per query, the same grid geometry as flash_fwd! (and the same tile-staging of k/v and of the key mask).\n\ndQ[:, js, i, bh] is written once, at the end, by the thread that owns js, no atomics. dBias is accumulated across the key-tile loop via _atomic_fadd!: the address is shared by every i (bias has no i axis), so nouter threads across different groups can land on it, but never more than nouter, queries and keys otherwise sole-own their contribution. dbias_t is the gradient of bias_t, i.e. in the transposed [n (queries), n (keys), bh] layout, which is what makes a SIMD group's 32 atomics land on 32 consecutive addresses.\n\nA dead key (masked, or past n) is skipped outright. Its staged mask term is shared by the whole group, so the branch is uniform — no divergence — and it saves the atomic as well as the arithmetic.\n\ndbias_t MUST be zeroed by the caller before launch; dq need not be (every active thread's location is fully overwritten, exactly as the forward writes o). With HB = false neither bias_t nor dbias_t is touched.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.flash_bwd_dq!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.flash_bwd_dv!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"flash_bwd_dv!(dv, q, k, bias, mask, dO, lse,\n    scale, n, nouter, nh, mheads, Val(T), Val(DIM), Val(TG), Val(KT), Val(HB), Val(HM))\n\nBackward pass, dV, one thread per KEY, streaming query tiles, the same geometry and masking rules as flash_bwd_dk!.\n\ndV[:, kk] = Σ_j p_{j,kk} dO_j needs only the softmax weight p, never dp and never delta. So this kernel takes NEITHER v NOR delta as an argument, they are absent from the signature rather than merely unused, so nothing binds a buffer the kernel never reads, and it holds only kv plus the dvv accumulator: 2*DIM registers, the same footprint as flash_fwd!.\n\nThat is what the split buys, and it is why dV rather than dK is the half worth separating; see the register-pressure note in the file header. The s/p recomputed here duplicates flash_bwd_dk!'s, which is pure ALU and repaid many times over by not spilling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.flash_bwd_dv!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.flash_config-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Integer, Integer}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"flash_config(T, dim, n) -> Dict(stage => (; tg, kt[, sb]))\n\nThe configuration each stage of the :default variant tuned at length n was (or would be) compiled with, from the calibration exactly as tune_flash_kernel! chose it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.flash_config"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.flash_fwd!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"flash_fwd!(o, lse, q, k, v, bias_t, mask, scale, neg_inf, n, nouter, nh, mheads,\n    Val(T), Val(DIM), Val(TG), Val(KT), Val(SB), Val(HB), Val(HM))\n\nFused attention forward: online softmax over key tiles, one thread per query.\n\nunsafe_indices=true drops KernelAbstractions' implicit __validindex guard. That is deliberate and safe: the kernel does its own bounds handling via active = j < n, and dropping the guard is what allows the sequence length to be a runtime argument rather than baked into the compiled code.\n\nThreads past the end must NOT return early, they still take part in the cooperative tile load and, critically, in the barriers. They are given a clamped index so every address stays in bounds and their result is simply never stored. This is what makes the kernel correct at ANY n, with no padding to a multiple of the tile size.\n\nAlgorithm\n\nPer key tile of KT keys, the group stages k, v and the tile's key mask into local memory; each thread then walks the tile in SUB-BLOCKS of SB keys. A sub-block's SB scores are formed first, their maximum updates the running maximum ONCE, and the accumulator is rescaled once — rather than once per key, which costs a full DIM-wide multiply and a second exponential per key. SB trades that saving against SB extra live registers and is tuned per device (see flash/tune.jl).\n\nSoftmax runs in base 2: scale·log2(e) is folded into q once and log2(e) into each bias value, so every exponential is a bare exp2. lse is converted back to the natural log on the way out.\n\nThe key mask is staged as an ADDITIVE term, 0 for a live key and neg_inf (a large FINITE negative, -floatmax) for a masked one or one past n, so the inner loop has no branch. A masked key then has p = exp2(≈ -floatmax - m) = 0 exactly wherever its row has a live key. A row whose leading keys are all masked accumulates finite garbage until its first live key arrives, and that key's correction factor exp2(≈ -floatmax - m_live) = 0 wipes it exactly. A row with NO live key never leaves m ≈ neg_inf: every score is neg_inf exactly (the real score is absorbed, |s| ≪ ulp(floatmax)), so every key, live or past n, has p = 1, and the accumulator holds Σₖ vₖ (keys past n stage v = 0). It is detected at the end and written as UNIFORM attention, o = Σₖ vₖ / n (the mean of v, what OpenFold's and Boltz2's finite -1e9 mask gives such a row; LuxLib's -Inf would give NaN) and lse = -Inf, the marker the backward reads. The branch is after the key loop, so the hot loop is the same code as without it.\n\nLayout\n\nn is the attended length (queries = keys) and nouter the OUTER length the attention is batched over. They are equal for triangle attention ([C, N, N, B]), and differ for everything else: MSA row attention attends over n = N_res for each of nouter = N_seq rows, column attention the reverse, and a plain [C, N, B] sequence has nouter = 1.\n\nq, k, v, o are [DIM, nh, n, nouter, B] with DIM fastest and element type T, where nh is the number of heads interleaved between the channel and query axes: nh = 1 is the packed [DIM, n, nouter, bh] case. bias_t is the pair bias TRANSPOSED, [n (queries), n (keys), bh] of T — QUERIES fastest, the registry's :bias_t role — so that the 32 threads of a SIMD group, which own 32 consecutive queries, read 32 consecutive elements for each key. In the keys-fastest :bias layout the same read touches 32 different cache lines; measured on an M1 Pro that single change was worth 1.3x of the forward. lse is [n, nh, nouter, B] of Float32 ([n, nouter, bh] when nh = 1). mask is [n, nouter, bh ÷ mheads] of Bool (a KEY mask per outer index, true = DROP), see mheads below; the common case is one mask per batch element, [n, nouter, B]. All raw pointers; see the note at the top of this file.\n\nPrecision\n\nT is storage only. scale, neg_inf, every accumulator and lse are Float32, see note 3 at the top of this file for why that is a requirement and not a default.\n\nAbsent bias or mask\n\nHB (has bias) and HM (has mask) are compile-time flags. HB = false drops the bias term from every score — one global load per (query, key), H·N³ over a triangle-attention call — and HM = false stages every in-range key as live without reading mask. The corresponding pointer is then never dereferenced, so a launcher binds any placeholder buffer: the ABI, and so the registry's buffer table, is the same for all four specialisations. HB = HM = true is the general kernel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.flash_fwd!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.flash_mma_bwd_kv!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"flash_mma_bwd_kv!(dk, dv, dbias, q, k, v, bias, mask, dO, lse, delta, scale, n, nouter, nh,\n    mheads, Val(BE), Val(T), Val(DIM), Val(NSG), Val(R), Val(BQ), Val(HB), Val(HM), Val(PE))\n\nThe dK/dV/dbias stage of :flash_mma. Rows are KEYS: each simdgroup holds R fragments of FM keys' K and V operands and walks the queries in tiles of BQ, forming Sᵀ = K·Qᵀ and dPᵀ = V·dOᵀ, then dV += Pᵀ·dO and dK += dSᵀ·Q. dbias (Float32, keys-fastest, ZEROED by the caller) is accumulated with atomics, because the bias has no outer axis. lse and delta are the forward's residual and Σ o·dO, both Float32, laid out as flash_fwd!'s lse.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.flash_mma_bwd_kv!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.flash_mma_bwd_q!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"flash_mma_bwd_q!(dq, q, k, v, bias, mask, dO, lse, delta, scale, n, nouter, nh, mheads,\n    Val(BE), Val(T), Val(DIM), Val(NSG), Val(R), Val(BK), Val(HB), Val(HM), Val(PE))\n\nThe dQ stage of :flash_mma: rows are queries, holding Q and dO fragments; per key tile, S = Q·Kᵀ, dP = dO·Vᵀ, dS = P∘(dP − delta), dQ += dS·K.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.flash_mma_bwd_q!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.flash_mma_config-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Type{T}, Integer, Integer}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"flash_mma_config(T, dim, n) -> Dict(stage => (; nsg, r, tile)): what tune_flash_mma! picks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.flash_mma_config"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.flash_mma_fwd!-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"flash_mma_fwd!(o, lse, q, k, v, bias, mask, scale, n, nouter, nh, mheads,\n    Val(BE), Val(T), Val(DIM), Val(NSG), Val(R), Val(BK), Val(HB), Val(HM), Val(PE))\n\nForward of :flash_mma. Same buffers, layout and results as flash_fwd! except that the pair bias is read in the ordinary keys-fastest :bias layout (each lane reads two adjacent keys of its own query row) rather than transposed. mask is the DROP mask [n, nouter, bh ÷ mheads].\n\nA group of 32·NSG threads covers FM·R·NSG queries; each simdgroup owns R fragments of FM query rows and holds their Q operands for the whole call. Per key tile of BK keys the group stages Kᵀ, V (in PE) and the additive key mask; see this file's header for the in-register softmax. Q·Kᵀ multiplies storage-type fragments; P·V multiplies PE operands; every accumulator is Float32 (see \"Precision\" in this file's header).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.flash_mma_fwd!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.flash_mma_source_tag-Tuple{}","page":"Internals","text":"flash_mma_source_tag() -> String\n\nShort hash of forward.jl and backward.jl, in every :flash_mma binary's name and in its registry manifest, so an edited kernel is rebuilt rather than served from a stale binary (see kernel_source_tag).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.flash_mma_source_tag"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.grid_points-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Union{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA, LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar}, Symbol, Integer, Type{T}, LuxTriangleAttention.DeviceCaps}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"grid_points(kernel, stage, dim, T, caps) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nThe admissible points of a stage's tune_grid, in grid order (first axis fastest).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.grid_points"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.kernel_argtypes-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Symbol, Type{T}, Integer, Any}, Tuple{Symbol, Type{T}, Integer, Any, Bool}, Tuple{Symbol, Type{T}, Integer, Any, Bool, Bool}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"kernel_argtypes(stage, T, dim, cfg, hasbias=true, hasmask=true) -> Tuple\n\nThe argument types of one stage's kernel AFTER KernelAbstractions' context, in signature order, for KernelDispatch.ka_compile: AbstractArray{X} for a buffer (bound as a raw device pointer to X), a bits type for a scalar, Val{x} for a compile-time parameter (no buffer slot, so the ABI is the same for every hasbias/hasmask combination). This is the contract the launch binds against: SDPA's forward/backward pass exactly these arguments, in this order.\n\nThe storage type T is used for q, k, v, o, dO and the bias; lse, delta and dbias are Float32, never narrowed; the mask is Bool. The four Int32s are n, nouter, nh, mheads.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.kernel_argtypes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.kernel_source_tag-Tuple{}","page":"Internals","text":"kernel_source_tag() -> String\n\nShort hash of the kernel sources, for a backend to put in its binary cache key.\n\nThe cache key that matters — the stage, T, dim and the tuned parameters — says what a binary was compiled FOR, not what it was compiled FROM. Without this, editing flash/forward.jl leaves every previously built binary on disk under a key that still matches, so the next run silently launches the OLD kernel: no error, no recompile, and results that quietly belong to code that no longer exists.\n\nComputed once, when the package is precompiled, over the kernel source files — so it tracks the code actually baked into the loaded image. It does NOT cover the toolchain (Julia, GPUCompiler, KernelAbstractions, or the backend's own compiler), which can also change codegen.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.kernel_source_tag"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.load_calibration-Tuple{Union{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA, LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar}, AbstractString}","page":"Internals","text":"load_calibration(kernel, path) -> Calibration or `nothing`\n\nRead a persisted calibration, or nothing if it is absent, unreadable, of another CALIBRATION_VERSION or kernel source, or missing a stage. A stale cache must never be fatal: the response is to measure again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.load_calibration"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.measure-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Union{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA, LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar}, Symbol, Type{T}, Integer, NamedTuple}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"measure(kernel, stage, T, dim, p; V, probe) -> Float64\n\nOne candidate: build it, check its pipeline admits its threads, verify it on V (once per session), then the GPU time of one launch on the probe (probe = (; P, n, nouter, heads)). Inf for a candidate that fails any step or throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.measure"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.mma_argtypes-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Symbol, Type{T}, Integer, Any, Bool, Bool, KernelDispatch.KernelBackend}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"mma_argtypes(stage, T, dim, p, hasbias, hasmask, backend::KernelBackend) -> Tuple\n\nThe argument types of one :flash_mma stage after KernelAbstractions' context, for KernelDispatch.ka_compile (see kernel_argtypes): the buffers in role order, the scale and the four Int32s, then the compile-time parameters, Val{typeof(backend)} first (the tag the fragment hooks dispatch on), Val{PE} last (the second products' operand type, cfg.ops; Float32 when the point has no ops).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.mma_argtypes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.mma_compute_type-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Type{T}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"mma_compute_type(be, T) -> Union{DataType,Nothing}\n\nThe storage type :flash_mma computes a call of storage type T at on this device: T itself (the default, Metal), or the type its matrix units take instead, to which the call's inputs are cast (saturating) and from which the results are cast back (CUDA: Float32 runs at Float16); nothing when there is none, and the call runs :default. See _narrow in sdpa_op.jl.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.mma_compute_type"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.mma_operand_types-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Any, Type{T}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"mma_operand_types(be, T) -> Vector{DataType}\n\nThe operand types ops of the second matrix products the backend can run for storage type T: Float32 and, for 16-bit storage, T itself (the default). A backend extension narrows it; an empty list means :flash_mma has no configuration for T there (CUDA, Float32).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.mma_operand_types"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.mma_ops-Tuple{Any}","page":"Internals","text":"mma_ops(p): the operand type of the second matrix products at point p (its ops, default Float32).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.mma_ops"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.mma_shmem-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Symbol, Integer, Type{T}, Any}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"mma_shmem(stage, dim, T, cfg) -> Int\n\nLocal memory one group of stage uses at cfg, in bytes (see each kernel's @localmem).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.mma_shmem"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.packed_problem-NTuple{9, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"packed_problem(q, k, v, bias, mask, dO, o, lse, delta) -> NamedTuple\n\nDevice buffers for one attention problem in the packed [dim, n, nouter, bh] layout (nh = 1), by the roles stage_args reads, plus zeroed outputs. o, lse and delta are INPUTS of the backward stages here, so each stage runs against fixed residuals rather than the output of whichever forward ran last. bias_t/dbias_t are the transposed pair-bias layouts :default reads/writes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.packed_problem"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.permute_ij_maybe-Tuple{Static.False, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"permute_ij_maybe(is_starting, x, [mask])\n\nSwaps the i and j dimensions of the input tensor(s) if is_starting is false.\n\nArguments\n\nis_starting: StaticBool. If false, triggers permutation.\nx: Input tensor.\nmask: Optional input mask.\n\nReturns\n\nPermuted (or original) tensor(s).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.permute_ij_maybe"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.reference_backward-NTuple{7, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"reference_backward(q, k, v, bias, mask, mheads, dO) -> (dq, dk, dv, dbias)\n\nThe analytic gradient of reference_forward, Float64, packed layout. dbias is keys-fastest [n, n, bh] and summed over the outer axis, as bias has none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.reference_backward"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.reference_forward-NTuple{6, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"reference_forward(q, k, v, bias, mask, mheads) -> (o, lse)\n\nPlain-loop attention in Float64, packed layout. A row with no live key attends uniformly: o is the mean of v over the n keys and lse = -Inf (the marker the backward reads), which is what the kernel is specified to write for it, and what a finite -1e9 mask gives in OpenFold and Boltz2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.reference_forward"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.run_stage!-Tuple{Union{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA, LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar}, Symbol, KernelDispatch.KernelBinary, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"run_stage!(kernel, stage, b, P; n, nouter, heads, mheads) -> outputs\n\nRun binary b (stage stage) on a packed problem P and return its outputs, in stage_outputs order. The outputs are zeroed first: the bias gradients accumulate.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.run_stage!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.save_calibration-Tuple{Union{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA, LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar}, AbstractString, Dict{Symbol, Dict{NamedTuple, Float64}}}","page":"Internals","text":"save_calibration(kernel, path, tref)\n\nPersist a calibration: per stage, its axes and one entry per finite point (values, time). Non-finite entries (points that failed to build, dispatch or verify) are dropped, as TOML cannot represent Inf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.save_calibration"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.search-Tuple{AbstractVector{<:NamedTuple}, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"search(points, evaluate) -> Dict{NamedTuple,Float64}\n\nEvaluate parameter points (all with the same axes) and return every point evaluated with its cost. evaluate(p) -> Float64 is Inf for a point that cannot run or is wrong. At most FULL_SWEEP_MAX points: all of them; more: coordinate descent over the axes from the middle point (see this file's header), falling back to a full sweep if nothing it reaches is finite.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.search"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.stage_args","page":"Internals","text":"stage_args(kernel, Val(stage), a, s) -> Tuple\n\nThe arguments of one built-in stage's binary, in slot order. a holds the buffers by role — o, lse, q, k, v, bias (keys fastest), bias_t (queries fastest), mask, dO, delta, dq, dk, dv, dbias, dbias_t — of which a stage reads only its own; an output may be an OutArray (allocated by the launch) or a preallocated array. s is (scale, n, nouter, nh, mheads) (_scalars for the op's own layout).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.stage_args"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.stage_kernel-Tuple{Symbol}","page":"Internals","text":"stage_kernel(stage) -> KernelAbstractions kernel function\n\nThe @kernel implementing one stage of the built-in entry: :fwd, :dq, :dk or :dv.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.stage_kernel"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.stage_launch-Tuple{Union{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA, LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar}, Symbol, KernelDispatch.KernelBinary, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"stage_launch(kernel, stage, b, P; n, nouter, heads, mheads) -> KernelDispatch.Launch\n\nOne launch of binary b (stage stage) on a packed problem P, prepared but not run: its arguments from stage_args (nh = 1), sized by extent = (n, nouter, heads).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.stage_launch"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.stage_outputs-Tuple{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar, Symbol}","page":"Internals","text":"stage_outputs(kernel, stage) -> Tuple{Vararg{Symbol}}\n\nThe roles one stage writes, in the order its binary returns them (the leading arguments of stage_args).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.stage_outputs"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.stage_shmem-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Symbol, Integer, Type{T}, Any}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"stage_shmem(stage, dim, T, p) -> Int\n\nLocal memory, in bytes, one group of stage uses: two staged [dim, kt] tiles in the storage type plus the per-key Float32 vectors (the forward's and :dq's staged mask, :dk's lse+delta, :dv's lse).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.stage_shmem"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.supported_eltypes-Tuple{}","page":"Internals","text":"supported_eltypes() -> Tuple{Vararg{DataType}}\n\nStorage element types the built-in kernels will run.\n\nThe kernels are written generically over the storage type (accumulation is always Float32 — see note 3 in src/kernels/flash/forward.jl). A dtype is listed only after the reference comparison has run at it: a path that has only ever been executed at Float32 is untested, not working. Float16 and BFloat16 (BFloat16s.jl's, the type Metal.jl and Reactant use) are checked against the reference on upcast inputs, on the eager and traced paths (test/kernels/launch.gpu.test.jl, test/ext/reactant/attention_kernel.test.jl). They are the precisions KernelDispatch 0.2 supports (eagerly, from a trace, and in launches that mix them), and its signature check refuses a launch whose buffers are not of the type the binary was compiled for.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.supported_eltypes"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.to_device-Tuple{KernelDispatch.KernelBackend, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"to_device(backend, x::Array) -> device array\n\nx copied to the backend's device. Implemented by the backend extension; the tuner's probe and verification problems are built on the host and moved with it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.to_device"},{"category":"function","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.tune!","page":"Internals","text":"tune!(kernel, lengths=tune_lengths(); dim, T=Float32, force=false) -> variants\n\nTune, compile and register a built-in kernel for each sequence length in lengths (an integer or a collection). For each length n:\n\ncalibrate the device at n for (dim, T), or reuse the persisted calibration (calibrate!);\npick, PER STAGE, the fastest configuration at n (choose_config);\ncompile every stage at its configuration, for every bias / mask presence (the absent-operand specialisations are binaries of their own, so a traced call without a bias or mask never has to compile), and register them with KernelDispatch.add_variant!, keyed (dtype, dim, n, hasbias, hasmask).\n\nA call of any length runs the variant tuned for the closest one (KernelDispatch.nearest). A length with a registered variant is skipped unless force (which also re-measures). The attention geometry (outer extent, heads) is not a key: on an M1 Pro, over triangle, MSA-row and plain-sequence shapes, the per-device choice costs at most ~10% against tuning each geometry (docs/done/FEATUREflashmma.md).\n\nSeconds to minutes per length on a cold calibration; run it deliberately, ahead of latency-sensitive work.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.tune!"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.tune_grid-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA, Symbol, Integer, Type{T}, LuxTriangleAttention.DeviceCaps}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"tune_grid(::FlashMMA, stage, dim, T, caps)\n\n2–16 simdgroups per group, one or two 8-row blocks per simdgroup, staged tiles of 16–64, and for 16-bit storage the operand type of the second matrix products, ops: Float32 or the storage type (see \"Precision\" in forward.jl). Each candidate is verified, so a 16-bit choice that is too inaccurate, or whose Float16 dS overflows on a large dO (DO_SCALE), is never picked.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.tune_grid"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.tune_grid-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar, Symbol, Integer, Type{T}, LuxTriangleAttention.DeviceCaps}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"tune_grid(::FlashScalar, stage, dim, T, caps)\n\nGroup sizes that are multiples of the SIMD width up to the device maximum, key tiles of 16–128, and for the forward a softmax sub-block of 1–8. Independent of n by construction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.tune_grid"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.tuned_config-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Union{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA, LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar}, Type{T}, Integer, Integer}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"tuned_config(kernel, T, dim, n) -> Dict(stage => point): what tune! picks at length n.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.tuned_config"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.verification_error-Tuple{Any, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"verification_error(device, reference) -> Float64\n\nmax|device - reference| / max|reference| over the entries where the reference is finite, in Float64. Inf for a non-finite device value there, so a NaN can never pass by comparing false against the bound. A reference entry that is itself infinite (the lse = -Inf marker of a fully masked row) must be reproduced EXACTLY, and counts as an error otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.verification_error"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.verification_problem-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Integer, Type{T}}} where T","page":"Internals","text":"verification_problem(dim, T; seed=0) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe fixed problem every candidate is checked on, with its reference results.\n\nSmall enough to cost milliseconds, shaped to reach every addressing path a configuration can get wrong: n = 67 is prime, so it is ragged against every tile and group size; nouter = 5 ≠ n exercises the rectangular strides; bh = 4 with mheads = 2 gives two batch elements with DIFFERENT masks, so a wrong batch stride on the mask is visible; the mask drops ~15% of keys and every row keeps at least one live key, except outer slice 2 of the FIRST batch mask, which drops every key (the uniform-attention path) while the second batch mask keeps it live. Operands are generated in Float64 and rounded to T once, and the reference is computed FROM the rounded values, so storage rounding is not counted as kernel error.\n\no, lse and delta are provided as inputs too: each backward stage is checked on its own against exact residuals, rather than inheriting whatever error the forward candidate happened to make.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.verification_problem"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.verification_tolerance-Tuple{Type{Float32}}","page":"Internals","text":"verification_tolerance(T) -> Float64\n\nLargest accepted max|device - reference| / max|reference| per output. Measured on the verification problem (:default, dim 16/32): Float32 ~5e-7, Float16 2–4e-4, BFloat16 2–4e-3 — the rounding of the outputs to the storage type. The bounds leave margin for reassociation and for 16-bit matrix operands (:flash_mma), and still catch any addressing or tiling error, which produces errors of order one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.verification_tolerance"},{"category":"method","location":"internals/#LuxTriangleAttention.verify_stage-Tuple{Union{LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA, LuxTriangleAttention.FlashScalar}, Symbol, KernelDispatch.KernelBinary, Any}","page":"Internals","text":"verify_stage(kernel, stage, b, V) -> Bool\n\nThe correctness gate: run binary b on verification_problem V and compare every output of the stage with the plain-loop reference, within verification_tolerance. A backward stage is also checked on a large dO (DO_SCALE).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.verify_stage"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/2-fused-kernels/#2.-Fused-attention-kernels","page":"Fused attention kernels","text":"The ordinary attention path computes the whole score tensor. For a pair representation that is O(N³·H) memory, and it is the part of a Pairformer block that does not fit at large N. A fused kernel tiles the keys inside the GPU kernel (the FlashAttention scheme) and never materialises the scores. This package ships two of them for Apple GPUs and the means to tune and replace them.","title":"2. Fused attention kernels"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/2-fused-kernels/#Turning-it-on","page":"Fused attention kernels","text":"using Lux, LuxTriangleAttention, Metal, Random\n\nattn = TriangleAttention(128, 32, 4; use_kernel=true)\nps, st = Lux.setup(Random.default_rng(), attn)\n\ndev = Lux.gpu_device()                           # Metal, once Metal.jl is loaded\nps, st = dev(ps), dev(st)\nx = dev(randn(Float32, 128, 256, 256, 1))\ny, st = attn(x, ps, st)\n\nuse_kernel is a property of the layer, so it is part of its type and the choice is made at compile time. It takes effect on device arrays. On the host a layer built with use_kernel=true runs the ordinary softmax and gives the same answer, so a model can be built once and run anywhere. It cannot be combined with chunk_size, and it needs self-attention-shaped q and k (windowed cross-attention with 32 queries against 128 keys is refused with a message, not mis-addressed).\n\nnote: No device kernel, no fallback\nA device array with no usable kernel is a configuration error and throws. Silently running host attention on a GPU would hide a 10–100× slowdown behind a correct answer.","title":"Turning it on"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/2-fused-kernels/#The-two-built-in-kernels","page":"Fused attention kernels","text":"Attention is one KernelDispatch op, SDPA, and the package registers two kernels for it:\n\nname what\n:default a KernelAbstractions flash kernel, one thread per query\n:flash_mma the matrix-unit variant (Apple simdgroup matrices), with a head dimension that is a multiple of 8\n\nlist_kernels()                 # the kernels, their variant counts and the selected one\ncurrent_kernel()               # :default\n\nuse_kernel!(:flash_mma)        # this session, from the next top-level statement\ncurrent_kernel()               # :flash_mma\n\nuse_kernel! is a method, not a table lookup, which keeps the op call type-stable. Two consequences follow from that:\n\nit takes effect from the next top-level statement. An attention call later in the same function or block throws instead of silently running the previous kernel;\nto run a single call with a given kernel and no global switch, call forward directly: KernelDispatch.forward(SDPA(), LuxTriangleAttention.FlashMMA(), q, k, v, mask, bias).\n\nTo change what runs by default in a project, persist the choice through KernelDispatch (it is stored as a package preference and applies from the next session):\n\nusing KernelDispatch\nset_default_kernel!(SDPA, :flash_mma)","title":"The two built-in kernels"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/2-fused-kernels/#Tuning","page":"Fused attention kernels","text":"A kernel's speed depends on the device, the storage type, the head dimension and the sequence length: the worst legal configuration can be 4–5× slower than the best. Each kernel is therefore tuned on your device. The first device call that finds no variant tunes the default lengths itself and says so, but it is better done deliberately, ahead of latency-sensitive work:\n\ntune_flash_kernel!(; dim=32)                    # :default,   lengths from tune_lengths(): 256 and 512\ntune_flash_mma!(; dim=32, T=Float16)            # :flash_mma, Float16 storage\ntune_flash_kernel!([768, 1024]; dim=32)         # also tune for larger proteins\nset_tune_lengths!([256, 512, 768, 1024])        # make that the project default (LocalPreferences.toml)\n\nWhat tuning does, for each length n:\n\nbuilds the admissible configurations of each stage (group size, key tile, softmax sub-block for :default; simdgroups, row blocks and staged tile for :flash_mma), searching the grid exhaustively up to 32 points and by coordinate descent beyond;\nchecks every candidate before its time counts: it must be admitted by its compiled pipeline and reproduce a plain-loop reference on a small problem. Over-dispatching a pipeline returns garbage and an impossibly fast time, which would otherwise read as a huge win;\ntimes the survivors on a triangle problem of exactly length n, and keeps the fastest per stage;\ncompiles every stage at that configuration for each combination of bias and mask being present (an absent operand is a binary of its own), and registers them as variants keyed (dtype, dim, n, hasbias, hasmask).\n\nA call of any length runs the variant tuned for the closest length. The attention geometry (outer extent, number of heads) is deliberately not part of the key: measured on an M1 Pro over triangle, MSA-row and plain-sequence shapes, one choice per device costs at most about 10 % against tuning every geometry.\n\nThe calibration (the measured times) is persisted next to the binaries, so repeating a tuning call is a file read:\n\ncalibrate!(; dim=32, n=512)          # measure :default at one length (also done by tune_flash_kernel!)\ncalibrate_mma!(; dim=32, n=512)      # the same for :flash_mma\nclear_calibration!(; disk=true)      # forget measurements, in memory and on disk, e.g. after editing a kernel\n\nEverything lives under ~/.cache/KernelDispatch/ops/LuxTriangleAttention.sdpa/<kernel>/. Set KERNELDISPATCH_CACHE to move the root (useful for tests and benchmarks that want an isolated cache). Variants built from other kernel sources are ignored and re-tuned on demand, so updating the package never serves a stale binary.","title":"Tuning"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/2-fused-kernels/#Precision","page":"Fused attention kernels","text":"Storage is Float32, Float16 or BFloat16; every accumulator, the softmax state, lse and delta are Float32 in both kernels. :flash_mma additionally tunes the operand type of the second matrix product of each step: Float32 or, for 16-bit storage, the storage type (the FlashAttention-2 convention). On an M1, BFloat16 operands fail verification (Apple's compiler cannot assemble a bfloat fragment from registers), so BFloat16 storage runs with Float32 operands there. See Half precision for the rest.\n\nk, v, the bias and the incoming gradient are converted to q's storage type, so mixed-precision operands run rather than being refused. A bias is converted with saturation at ±floatmax(T)/2: a mask folded into the bias as 1e9·(mask - 1) is finite in Float32 but -Inf in Float16, and would turn a row into NaN.","title":"Precision"},{"category":"section","location":"tutorials/2-fused-kernels/#Gradients","page":"Fused attention kernels","text":"Both kernels have a backward, and the layer is differentiable through it:\n\nEager Enzyme on device arrays uses KernelDispatch' one rule on forward, which runs backward. Enzyme cannot differentiate the ordinary Metal.jl code around it, so on Apple hardware training goes through Reactant.\nUnder Reactant the forward is a custom call in the compiled program. A gradient traced through a kernel throws until Enzyme-JAX can differentiate a custom call (EnzymeAD/Enzyme#2516); use use_kernel=false for differentiated traced programs, and see Compiling with Reactant.","title":"Gradients"},{"category":"section","location":"#LuxTriangleAttention.jl","page":"Home","text":"Triangle Attention and Triangle Multiplication layers for Lux.jl, built for protein structure prediction models such as AlphaFold and Boltz.\n\nTriangleAttention: starting and ending node attention with a triangle bias (AlphaFold2 Algorithms 13 and 14).\nTriangleMultiplication: outgoing and incoming triangle multiplication (AlphaFold2 Algorithms 11 and 12).\nAttention: the multi-head attention both are built on, usable on its own (self- and cross-attention, gating, pair bias, masks).\nFused attention kernels for Apple GPUs, tuned for your device, that run eagerly and inside a Reactant-compiled program.\nFloat16 and BFloat16 that stay finite on real activations.\nNumerically verified against the PyTorch references of AlphaFold2, AlphaFold3 and Boltz2.","title":"LuxTriangleAttention.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(\"LuxTriangleAttention\")\n\nJulia 1.12 is required. The GPU kernels need Metal.jl (load it with using Metal), and compiled programs need Reactant.jl; both are optional and enable package extensions.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-start","page":"Home","text":"Inputs are channels-first: [C, Ni, Nj, B].\n\nusing Lux, LuxTriangleAttention, Random\n\nx = randn(Float32, 64, 32, 32, 1)\n\nattn = TriangleAttention(64, 32, 4; is_starting=true)     # channels, head dimension, heads\nps, st = Lux.setup(Random.default_rng(), attn)\ny, st = attn(x, ps, st)                                   # [64, 32, 32, 1]\n\nmul = TriangleMultiplication(64, 128; is_outgoing=true)   # channels, hidden channels\nps, st = Lux.setup(Random.default_rng(), mul)\ny, st = mul(x, ps, st)\n\nThe first tutorial covers the layers, masks and the bias layouts in detail.","title":"Quick start"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":" \nLayers, masks and bias the layers, their shapes, boolean masks, prep_bias and the :qk / :kq bias layouts\nFused attention kernels use_kernel, the two built-in kernels, tuning and persisting the result\nCompiling with Reactant @compile, chunk_size and why the chunk loop must stay rolled\nHalf precision Float16 and BFloat16: what overflows, and the three contraction modes\nYour own attention kernel registering a .metallib or .cubin as an attention kernel\nKernel operands the shapes, element types and argument lists every attention kernel reads\nAPI reference every exported and public name","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"#Dimension-conventions","page":"Home","text":"Attention here runs over the third dimension (token_dim=3) of a [C, Ni, Nj, B] input, with the queries [head_dim, H, Ni, Nj, B]. Many Python implementations (AlphaFold, Boltz) attend over the fourth dimension of a [B, S, N, C] tensor, so a Python bias or mask needs the permutation that prep_bias and prep_mask apply. The two bias layouts exist for exactly this.","title":"Dimension conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"#Kernels-are-KernelDispatch-ops","page":"Home","text":"The kernel machinery (selection, variants, launching binaries, embedding a kernel in a Reactant program, the Enzyme rule) is KernelDispatch.jl. Attention is one op there, SDPA, and the built-in kernels are two of its kernels. Bringing your own is the last tutorial; the KernelDispatch documentation explains the concepts it builds on.","title":"Kernels are KernelDispatch ops"},{"category":"section","location":"#Backends","page":"Home","text":"Layers: ordinary Julia on any array type Lux supports, on the host or a GPU, and compiled with Reactant (CPU, or an Apple GPU through the jax-mps PJRT plugin).\nFused kernels: :default and :flash_mma run on Metal. On the host, use_kernel=true runs the ordinary softmax attention, so a model built for the GPU still runs on the CPU. On any other device the kernel has to be your own (a .cubin, say); KernelDispatch supports CUDA, but this package does not build kernels for it.","title":"Backends"}]
}
